// memdb.zm -- a small SQL engine in pure Ziman, holding its tables in memory, for tests.
//
// A `db.Driver` like `sqlite`, `pg` and `mysql`, so `datasource` reaches it the same way and
// nothing above it knows the difference:
//
//     val drivers = datasource.registry([memdb.driver()])
//     val conn = try datasource.open(drivers, "memdb:")
//     try migrate.run(conn, "migrations")          // or run schema.sql and data.sql by hand
//
// **It is a third target, not a replacement.** A test that passes here and fails on PostgreSQL
// certifies nothing, so the point of this engine is to run the *same* suite that sqlite and
// postgres run, where a divergence shows up as a failure instead of hiding as a false green. The
// divergences it knows about are listed at the bottom of this comment; run the real engine before
// believing anything surprising.
//
// ## What it understands
//
// `CREATE TABLE` (with `NOT NULL`, `PRIMARY KEY`, `UNIQUE`, `CHECK`, `DEFAULT` and a column type
// that is recorded but not enforced), `CREATE [UNIQUE] INDEX`, `ALTER TABLE ... ADD [COLUMN]`,
// `DROP TABLE [IF EXISTS]`, `INSERT INTO ... VALUES`, `SELECT [DISTINCT]` with `WHERE`,
// `GROUP BY`, `HAVING`, `ORDER BY`, `LIMIT` and `OFFSET`, `UPDATE ... SET ... WHERE`,
// `DELETE FROM ... WHERE`, `RETURNING` on any of the three writes, and
// `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK`.
//
// Set operations: `UNION [ALL]`, `INTERSECT [ALL]` and `EXCEPT [ALL]`, chained, with the
// compound's own `ORDER BY`, `LIMIT` and `OFFSET`. A compound's `ORDER BY` may name an output
// column or its position and nothing else, because there is no single source to resolve anything
// else against. Two of the five divergences between the two readings live here -- how a chain
// groups, and whether the `ALL` forms exist at all.
//
// `ORDER BY 2` and `ORDER BY alias` work everywhere, not only in a compound. They did not before
// set operations needed them: an integer term was evaluated per row, so `ORDER BY 1 DESC` compared
// 1 with 1 for every pair and quietly left the rows as they came, and an output name was "no such
// column". Five such queries now agree with SQLite that did not, including the out-of-range
// refusal, which is SQLite's wording down to the `1st`.
//
// `RETURNING a, b`, `RETURNING *` or an expression with an alias, on `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and
// `DELETE`. A qualified star, `t.*`, in a select list -- naming one table's columns by the name or
// alias the `FROM` gave it, which is what makes `p.*` work and `patient.*` not once `patient AS p`
// has been written. In a `RETURNING` clause it is one of the two readings' divergences, below. An `INSERT` answers what it stored with defaults filled in, an `UPDATE` the row as it
// now stands, a `DELETE` the row as it was -- each what a real engine answers. A `*` expands
// anywhere in the list, so `RETURNING *, id` is two copies of `id`, and the same is now true of a
// select list.
//
// **`RETURNING` is not ANSI SQL and both readings run it anyway.** The standard's form is a data
// change delta table -- `SELECT * FROM FINAL TABLE (INSERT ..)`, SQL:2011 -- which essentially only
// Db2 implements; `RETURNING` is PostgreSQL's, SQLite has had it since 3.35 and MySQL has nothing
// of the kind. So whether a connection has it is a question about the *engine*, which
// `datasource.dialect` answers, and not about which reading of SQL's semantics is in force. **A
// caller that gates on the dialect has to name `memdb` in its list** -- the sample application's
// `returns_written_row` does -- or its tests take a path production does not.
//
// Joins: `[INNER] JOIN`, `LEFT [OUTER] JOIN` and `CROSS JOIN`, with `ON` or `USING`, chained left
// to right and with table aliases. `RIGHT` and `FULL` are read by `sql` and refused here, with an
// error naming the engine to use instead -- they are a mirror and a union of what this does, and
// shipping them without a test that proves them against a real engine would be guessing.
//
// Subqueries: a scalar `(SELECT ..)`, `IN (SELECT ..)`, `NOT IN (SELECT ..)` and `EXISTS`, each of
// which may be correlated -- a name a subquery does not have resolves outward through the scopes
// enclosing it, which is what makes `WHERE visit.patient_id = patient.id` inside one mean what it
// says.
//
// Expressions: literals, column references (qualified or not), bound parameters (`?`, `$1`),
// `AND`/`OR`/`NOT`, the six comparisons, `IS [NOT] NULL`, `LIKE`, `IN`, `BETWEEN`, `CASE WHEN`,
// `CAST(x AS type)`, `+ - * /`, `||`, parentheses, the scalar functions `substr`, `length`,
// `lower`, `upper`, `trim`, `abs` and `coalesce`, and the aggregates `COUNT` (including
// `COUNT(DISTINCT x)`), `SUM`, `MIN`, `MAX` and `AVG`.
//
// **Constraints are enforced**, not merely remembered: `NOT NULL`, `PRIMARY KEY`, `UNIQUE` and
// `CHECK`, at column level and at table level, on `INSERT` and on `UPDATE`, with SQLite's own
// messages. Each reads NULL the way SQL says to, and the three readings differ: `NOT NULL` refuses
// a NULL; `UNIQUE` and `PRIMARY KEY` *ignore* a key with a NULL in it, because uniqueness asks
// whether two keys are equal and a NULL is equal to nothing; and a `CHECK` fails only when it is
// false, NULL being unknown and passing. A statement that violates a constraint leaves the
// database exactly as it was, which costs nothing here because a `Database` is a value.
//
// A single-column `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` is **SQLite's rowid under another name**: a NULL in it, or
// leaving the column out, takes the next value rather than being refused. The declared type must be
// exactly `INTEGER` -- `INT` and `BIGINT` are ordinary columns in SQLite and keep the NULL, which
// was measured rather than assumed. PostgreSQL has no rowid, so the strict reading does none of
// this and refuses a NULL in a key.
//
// **Columns are resolved before a statement runs**, as a real engine resolves them when it
// prepares one. Looking them up per row instead meant a query whose `WHERE` matched nothing never
// looked, so `SELECT count(age) FROM visit WHERE reason = 'nothing'` answered 0 where SQLite
// answers "no such column: age" -- a typo passing here and failing there is the exact false green
// this engine exists to avoid, and the cross-check fixture caught it within a minute of the
// aggregates being written. It caught the same thing a second time when joins arrived: validation
// had been loosened to let a correlated name through, which let every typo through with it, so it
// now checks against the shape the `FROM` produces *and* the scopes enclosing it -- which is also
// where an unqualified `id` across two joined tables becomes "ambiguous column name" rather than
// silently the left one.
//
// ## Two readings of SQL
//
// `driver()` at `memdb:` reads SQL the way SQLite does. `strict_driver()` at `memdb-ansi:` reads it
// the way the standard does, and the two differ in exactly nine places -- `LIKE`'s case
// sensitivity, what division by zero answers, whether a number may be compared with text, whether
// a boolean in a select list comes back as a boolean or as the integer 0 or 1 (SQLite has no
// boolean type; PostgreSQL and the standard do), whether a `RETURNING` clause may use a qualified
// star, **how a chain of set operations groups**, whether `INTERSECT ALL`, `EXCEPT ALL` and
// `UNION DISTINCT` exist at all, whether a `PRIMARY KEY` column may hold NULL, and whether a
// `UNIQUE` column may be added to a table that already has rows.
//
// The grouping is the one to remember, because nothing in the text of the query says which reading
// is in force and both answer rows. SQLite reads `UNION`, `INTERSECT` and `EXCEPT` strictly left to
// right; the standard and PostgreSQL bind `INTERSECT` tighter than the other two. So
// `a UNION b INTERSECT c` is `(a UNION b) INTERSECT c` on one engine and `a UNION (b INTERSECT c)`
// on the other -- a different answer, no error, no warning. Parenthesise it or do not write it.
//
// **Four of the nine run the other way**, and they are the ones where SQLite is the stricter
// engine: `RETURNING t.*`, the `ALL` forms of `INTERSECT` and `EXCEPT`, `UNION DISTINCT` and
// `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN .. UNIQUE` are each refused by SQLite and answered by PostgreSQL. There the lax reading refuses and the strict
// one runs it, so a statement failing on `memdb:` and passing on `memdb-ansi:` is PostgreSQL-only
// SQL that will break on SQLite. Both directions are worth knowing; only the direction of the
// arrow changes.
//
// **The strict one is a portability check.** A query that passes on `memdb:` and fails on
// `memdb-ansi:` is leaning on SQLite and will behave differently on PostgreSQL, which matters when
// one application ships on both. `tests/lang/memdb_ansi_strictness.zm` holds the list and asserts
// both halves: the lax reading still matches real SQLite, and the strict reading still differs.
//
// Measured against the sample application before any of it was written: its 191 SQL strings use no
// joins and no subqueries, because its repositories query one table each and compose in Ziman. The
// 20,365 characters of SQL in it are almost all of the shape above. Joins and subqueries are here
// anyway, because a test suite is not restricted to the queries the application happens to issue
// and an engine that refuses them would push every such test onto a container.
//
// Referential integrity, derived tables in a `FROM`, a parenthesised arm of a compound (which
// SQLite also refuses, so nothing is lost yet) and per-test forking are the next increments. `count(t.*)` is not read: SQLite refuses it,
// PostgreSQL counts whole rows with it, and nothing needs it. Anything it does not understand
// is an error naming the token, never a quiet partial execution.
//
// ## Transactions are free, because a database is a value
//
// A `Database` is an immutable `Map` of immutable tables, so `BEGIN` keeps the current one,
// `ROLLBACK` puts it back and `COMMIT` forgets it. There is no undo log and no copying: the saved
// database *is* the old state. That is also what will make per-test forking free later -- seed a
// schema and a dataset once, then hand every test its own copy of the same value.
//
// ## Known divergences from SQLite and PostgreSQL
//
//   - **No type affinity.** A column declared `INTEGER` holds whatever is inserted; SQLite would
//     coerce `'7'` to 7. Insert the right type.
//   - **The statement tree and the parser live in `sql`**, not here. This module is the engine:
//     storage, evaluation and the two drivers. `sql.parse` reads SQL, `sql.format` writes it back,
//     and anything wanting to read or rewrite SQL without a database uses that module alone.
//   - **A `FOREIGN KEY` is modelled and not yet enforced.** `NOT NULL`, `PRIMARY KEY`, `UNIQUE`
//     and `CHECK` are enforced; referential integrity is the next increment, so a row pointing at
//     a parent that is not there is accepted here and refused by both real engines (SQLite only
//     with `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON`, which this library's driver sets).
//   - **A column constraint with a name** (`a INT CONSTRAINT k CHECK (..)`) is kept as text and
//     not enforced, where the same constraint written at table level is. Nothing of it is lost; it
//     is simply opaque, and `sql`'s header says which shapes are modelled.
//   - **`CREATE INDEX` is accepted and ignored.** An index changes how fast a query runs, not what
//     it answers, and this engine has no query planner to put one in.
//   - **NULLs sort first ascending**, which is SQLite's order. PostgreSQL sorts them last. A test
//     that depends on either is already engine-specific.
//   - **No `sqlite_master`, no `information_schema`.** A test that inspects the schema through SQL
//     is asking the engine about itself, and this one answers only about data.

#![experimental]
use db.Conn
use db.DbError
use db.Driver
use db.Row
use db.SqlValue
use locale
use sql
use sql.AlterAction
use sql.AlterStmt
use sql.Assignment
use sql.BinNode
use sql.CallNode
use sql.Column
use sql.ConstraintRule
use sql.CreateStmt
use sql.DeleteStmt
use sql.DropStmt
use sql.Expr
use sql.InsertStmt
use sql.OrderTerm
use sql.SelectItem
use sql.SelectStmt
use sql.SetOp
use sql.Statement
use sql.TableConstraint
use sql.UpdateStmt

// Case folding for keywords and identifiers is protocol folding, so it names the invariant locale
// rather than whatever a program set as its default: in Turkish, `I` folds to `ı` and `SELECT`
// would stop being a keyword.
val invariant: Locale = locale.root()

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- storage

// A table: its columns in declaration order, and its rows as arrays of the same width. Row order
// is insertion order, which is what a `SELECT` with no `ORDER BY` returns -- the real engines
// promise nothing there, so a test that cares must say `ORDER BY`.
struct Table {
    name: string
    columns: [Column]
    rows: [[SqlValue]]
    // Every constraint this table carries, in **one** list: the table-level ones, the ones written
    // in a column's position, and the single-column `PRIMARY KEY`/`UNIQUE` flags turned into
    // constraints of their own. Three places to remember is how a constraint gets enforced on
    // insert and forgotten on update.
    constraints: [TableConstraint]
}

// The whole database as one immutable value. Tables are keyed by their folded name, so `Users` and
// `users` are one table, as they are in SQLite and (for unquoted names) PostgreSQL.
struct Database {
    tables: Map<string, Table>
}

fn empty_database() -> Database {
    return Database { tables: Map<string, Table> {} }
}

fn problem(message: string) -> DbError {
    return DbError.Sql(message)
}
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- evaluating

// What an expression is evaluated against: the row's shape and values, the statement's bound
// parameters, and whether this connection is the strict one.
//
// **`strict` is the whole of the ANSI-versus-SQLite difference**, and it is one field rather than
// two engines because the disagreements are few and local: `LIKE`'s case sensitivity, what
// division by zero answers, and whether a number may be compared with text. Each is marked
// `strict` at the point it decides, so the two readings cannot drift apart in separate code.
struct Ctx {
    columns: [string]
    // The table each column came from, by alias where one was given. A join puts two tables'
    // columns in one row, so `id` may be two columns and `o.id` only one -- which is what makes
    // an unqualified name ambiguous rather than simply wrong.
    tables: [string]
    row: [SqlValue]
    params: [SqlValue]
    strict: bool
    // The database, because a subquery is an expression that runs a query.
    database: Database
    // The scopes enclosing this one, outermost last. A correlated subquery reads a column of the
    // row its outer query is on, so resolution falls outward when a name is not here. Empty for a
    // query that is not nested; one deep per level of nesting.
    outer: [Ctx]
}

fn ctx_of(
    database: Database,
    columns: [string],
    tables: [string],
    row: [SqlValue],
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool
) -> Ctx {
    val none: [Ctx] = []
    return Ctx {
        columns: columns,
        tables: tables,
        row: row,
        params: params,
        strict: strict,
        database: database,
        outer: none
    }
}

// A context for a row with no table behind it: an `INSERT`'s values, a `LIMIT`, a `SELECT` with no
// `FROM`.
fn bare_ctx(database: Database, params: [SqlValue], strict: bool) -> Ctx {
    val no_names: [string] = []
    val no_row: [SqlValue] = []
    return ctx_of(database, no_names, no_names, no_row, params, strict)
}

fn nested_in(inner: Ctx, outer: Ctx) -> Ctx {
    return Ctx {
        columns: inner.columns,
        tables: inner.tables,
        row: inner.row,
        params: inner.params,
        strict: inner.strict,
        database: inner.database,
        outer: push(outer.outer, outer)
    }
}

// Where `ref` lives in this row: an index, or -1 for no such column and -2 for an ambiguous one.
fn resolve_column(cx: Ctx, ref: sql.ColRef) -> int {
    val want = sql.fold_name(ref.name)
    val qualifier = sql.fold_name(ref.table)
    var found = 0 - 1
    var count = 0
    for i in 0..prelude.len(cx.columns) {
        if sql.fold_name(cx.columns[i]) == want {
            if ref.table == "" || sql.fold_name(cx.tables[i]) == qualifier {
                found = i
                count = count + 1
            }
        }
    }
    if count > 1 {
        return 0 - 2
    }
    return found
}

// `ref`'s value, looked for in this scope and then outward through the enclosing ones.
fn lookup_column(cx: Ctx, ref: sql.ColRef) -> Result<SqlValue, DbError> {
    val at = resolve_column(cx, ref)
    if at == 0 - 2 {
        return err(problem(concat("ambiguous column name: ", ref.name)))
    }
    if at >= 0 {
        return ok(cx.row[at])
    }
    val depth = prelude.len(cx.outer)
    if depth > 0 {
        // Outermost last, so the nearest enclosing scope is the one at the end.
        return lookup_column(cx.outer[depth - 1], ref)
    }
    val written = if ref.table == "" { ref.name } else { concat(ref.table, ".", ref.name) }
    return err(problem(concat("no such column: ", written)))
}

// SQLite's type order, which is also the order NULLs sort first in: NULL, then numbers, then
// text, then blobs. PostgreSQL would refuse to compare a number with text at all; a test that
// relies on either is already engine-specific.
fn type_rank(v: SqlValue) -> int {
    return switch v {
        Null { 0 }
        Bool(_) { 1 }
        Int(_) { 1 }
        Float(_) { 1 }
        Text(_) { 2 }
        Bytes(_) { 3 }
    }
}

fn as_number(v: SqlValue) -> float {
    return switch v {
        Int(n) { n as float }
        Float(f) { f }
        Bool(b) { if b { 1.0 } else { 0.0 } }
        Null { 0.0 }
        Text(_) { 0.0 }
        Bytes(_) { 0.0 }
    }
}

// Negative, zero or positive, for `ORDER BY` and for the comparison operators. Two NULLs compare
// equal *here* so that sorting is a total order; `=` never reaches this with a NULL, because
// `compare_values` is only asked after the three-valued check below.
fn compare_values(a: SqlValue, b: SqlValue) -> int {
    val ra = type_rank(a)
    val rb = type_rank(b)
    if ra != rb {
        return if ra < rb { 0 - 1 } else { 1 }
    }
    return switch a {
        Text(sa) { switch b {
            Text(sb) { compare(sa, sb) }
            Null { 0 }
            Int(_) { 0 }
            Float(_) { 0 }
            Bool(_) { 0 }
            Bytes(_) { 0 }
        } }
        Bytes(ba) { switch b {
            Bytes(bb) { compare(ba, bb) }
            Null { 0 }
            Int(_) { 0 }
            Float(_) { 0 }
            Bool(_) { 0 }
            Text(_) { 0 }
        } }
        Null { 0 }
        Int(_) { compare(as_number(a), as_number(b)) }
        Float(_) { compare(as_number(a), as_number(b)) }
        Bool(_) { compare(as_number(a), as_number(b)) }
    }
}

// `WHERE` keeps a row when the filter is true; NULL and false both drop it, which is SQL's
// three-valued logic seen from the outside.
fn is_true(v: SqlValue) -> bool {
    return switch v {
        Bool(b) { b }
        Int(n) { n != 0 }
        Float(f) { f != 0.0 }
        Text(_) { false }
        Bytes(_) { false }
        Null { false }
    }
}

fn is_null(v: SqlValue) -> bool {
    return SqlValue.is_Null(v)
}

fn column_index(columns: [string], name: string) -> int {
    val want = sql.fold_name(name)
    for i in 0..prelude.len(columns) {
        if sql.fold_name(columns[i]) == want {
            return i
        }
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

fn eval_expr(e: Expr, cx: Ctx) -> Result<SqlValue, DbError> {
    return switch e {
        Lit(v) { ok(v) }
        Star { err(problem("'*' is only allowed in a select list or a RETURNING clause")) }
        TableStar(table) {
            err(problem(concat("'", table, ".*' is only allowed in a select list or a RETURNING clause")))
        }
        Param(number) {
            if number > prelude.len(cx.params) {
                err(problem(prelude.format(
                    "the statement wants parameter ${} but {} were given",
                    number,
                    prelude.len(cx.params)
                )))
            } else {
                ok(cx.params[number - 1])
            }
        }
        Col(ref) { lookup_column(cx, ref) }
        Sub(node) {
            // A scalar subquery: one row of one column, or NULL when it answers none. More than
            // one row is an error, as it is in both real engines -- a query that sometimes answers
            // two rows and sometimes one would otherwise be a bug that only shows on live data.
            val rows = try run_subquery(node.query, cx)
            if prelude.len(rows) == 0 {
                ok(SqlValue.Null)
            } else if prelude.len(rows) > 1 {
                err(problem(prelude.format(
                    "a subquery used as a value answered {} rows; it must answer at most one",
                    prelude.len(rows)
                )))
            } else if prelude.len(rows[0].values) != 1 {
                err(problem(prelude.format(
                    "a subquery used as a value answered {} columns; it must answer one",
                    prelude.len(rows[0].values)
                )))
            } else {
                ok(rows[0].values[0])
            }
        }
        Exists(node) {
            val rows = try run_subquery(node.query, cx)
            ok(SqlValue.Bool(prelude.len(rows) > 0))
        }
        InSub(node) {
            val subject = try eval_expr(node.subject, cx)
            if is_null(subject) {
                ok(SqlValue.Null)
            } else {
                val rows = try run_subquery(node.query, cx)
                var hit = false
                var unknown = false
                for r in rows {
                    if prelude.len(r.values) != 1 {
                        return err(problem("a subquery on the right of IN must answer one column"))
                    }
                    if is_null(r.values[0]) {
                        unknown = true
                    } else if compare_values(subject, r.values[0]) == 0 {
                        hit = true
                    }
                }
                if hit {
                    ok(SqlValue.Bool(!node.negated))
                } else if unknown {
                    ok(SqlValue.Null)
                } else {
                    ok(SqlValue.Bool(node.negated))
                }
            }
        }
        Cast(node) {
            val v = try eval_expr(node.inner, cx)
            cast_value(v, node.type_name, cx.strict)
        }
        Not(node) {
            val inner = try eval_expr(node.inner, cx)
            // NOT NULL is NULL, not true.
            if is_null(inner) { ok(SqlValue.Null) } else { ok(SqlValue.Bool(!is_true(inner))) }
        }
        IsNull(node) {
            val inner = try eval_expr(node.inner, cx)
            ok(SqlValue.Bool(if node.negated { !is_null(inner) } else { is_null(inner) }))
        }
        Bin(node) { eval_binary(node, cx) }
        Call(node) { eval_call(node, cx) }
        Like(node) {
            val subject = try eval_expr(node.subject, cx)
            val pattern = try eval_expr(node.pattern, cx)
            if is_null(subject) || is_null(pattern) {
                ok(SqlValue.Null)
            } else {
                val hit = like_matches(text_of(subject), text_of(pattern), cx.strict)
                ok(SqlValue.Bool(if node.negated { !hit } else { hit }))
            }
        }
        In(node) {
            val subject = try eval_expr(node.subject, cx)
            if is_null(subject) {
                ok(SqlValue.Null)
            } else {
                var hit = false
                var unknown = false
                for option in node.options {
                    val v = try eval_expr(option, cx)
                    if is_null(v) {
                        unknown = true
                    } else if compare_values(subject, v) == 0 {
                        hit = true
                    }
                }
                // `x IN (1, NULL)` is true when x is 1 and unknown otherwise -- a NULL in the list
                // cannot make a match false, only an absence unknown.
                if hit {
                    ok(SqlValue.Bool(!node.negated))
                } else if unknown {
                    ok(SqlValue.Null)
                } else {
                    ok(SqlValue.Bool(node.negated))
                }
            }
        }
        Between(node) {
            val subject = try eval_expr(node.subject, cx)
            val low = try eval_expr(node.low, cx)
            val high = try eval_expr(node.high, cx)
            if is_null(subject) || is_null(low) || is_null(high) {
                ok(SqlValue.Null)
            } else {
                val inside = compare_values(subject, low) >= 0 && compare_values(subject, high) <= 0
                ok(SqlValue.Bool(if node.negated { !inside } else { inside }))
            }
        }
        Case(node) {
            var answer = SqlValue.Null
            var decided = false
            for arm in node.arms {
                if !decided {
                    // `try` cannot sit on the right of `&&` -- it must run exactly once and
                    // unconditionally -- so the condition is evaluated in its own step.
                    val test = try eval_expr(arm.condition, cx)
                    if is_true(test) {
                        answer = try eval_expr(arm.value, cx)
                        decided = true
                    }
                }
            }
            if !decided && prelude.len(node.fallback) == 1 {
                answer = try eval_expr(node.fallback[0], cx)
            }
            ok(answer)
        }
    }
}

// SQL's `LIKE`: `%` stands for any run of characters, `_` for exactly one, and `\` escapes either.
//
// **Case-insensitive for ASCII, which is SQLite's rule and not PostgreSQL's** (there, `LIKE` is
// case-sensitive and `ILIKE` is the other one). The two real engines already disagree here, so a
// test that depends on it is engine-specific whatever this does; matching SQLite keeps `memdb`
// shadowing the engine it stands in for.
fn like_matches(subject: string, pattern: string, strict: bool) -> bool {
    if strict {
        // The standard compares characters under the collation, which for these purposes means
        // case-sensitively -- PostgreSQL's reading, and `ILIKE` is its separate insensitive form.
        return like_from(subject, 0, pattern, 0)
    }
    return like_from(to_lower(subject, invariant), 0, to_lower(pattern, invariant), 0)
}

fn like_from(s: string, si: int, p: string, pi: int) -> bool {
    var i = si
    var j = pi
    while j < prelude.len(p) {
        val pc = char_at(p, j)
        if pc == "%" {
            // Try every split, shortest first. A pattern of all `%` therefore costs one step, and
            // the backtracking only pays where a `%` is followed by something real.
            var k = i
            while k <= prelude.len(s) {
                if like_from(s, k, p, j + 1) {
                    return true
                }
                k = k + 1
            }
            return false
        }
        if i >= prelude.len(s) {
            return false
        }
        if pc == "_" {
            i = i + 1
            j = j + 1
            continue
        }
        if pc == "\\" && j + 1 < prelude.len(p) {
            if char_at(s, i) != char_at(p, j + 1) {
                return false
            }
            i = i + 1
            j = j + 2
            continue
        }
        if char_at(s, i) != pc {
            return false
        }
        i = i + 1
        j = j + 1
    }
    return i == prelude.len(s)
}

// The scalar functions this increment carries. `substr` and `length` are here because the sample
// application's own migrations call them 30 times each for a data conversion, and an engine that
// cannot run a real migration directory is not usable for the thing it was built for. The
// aggregates (`COUNT`, `SUM`, `MIN`, `MAX`) are the next increment and say so: an unknown name is
// an error, never a NULL.
fn eval_call(node: CallNode, cx: Ctx) -> Result<SqlValue, DbError> {
    try check_call(node)
    val name = sql.fold_name(node.name)
    val args = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
    for a in node.args {
        push(args, try eval_expr(a, cx))
    }
    val given = snapshot(args)
    val count = prelude.len(given)
    if name == "coalesce" {
        for v in given {
            if !is_null(v) {
                return ok(v)
            }
        }
        return ok(SqlValue.Null)
    }
    // Every other function here answers NULL for a NULL argument, as both real engines do.
    for v in given {
        if is_null(v) {
            return ok(SqlValue.Null)
        }
    }
    if name == "length" {
        return switch given[0] {
            Bytes(b) { ok(SqlValue.Int(prelude.len(b))) }
            Text(text) { ok(SqlValue.Int(prelude.len(text))) }
            Int(n) { ok(SqlValue.Int(prelude.len(str(n)))) }
            Float(f) { ok(SqlValue.Int(prelude.len(str(f)))) }
            Bool(_) { ok(SqlValue.Int(1)) }
            Null { ok(SqlValue.Null) }
        }
    }
    if name == "substr" || name == "substring" {
        val text = text_of(given[0])
        val size = prelude.len(text)
        // SQL counts from 1, and a negative start counts back from the end.
        var from = unwrap_int(given[1])
        if from < 0 {
            from = size + from + 1
        }
        if from < 1 {
            from = 1
        }
        var upto = size
        if count == 3 {
            val wanted = unwrap_int(given[2])
            if wanted < 0 {
                return ok(SqlValue.Text(""))
            }
            upto = from - 1 + wanted
        }
        if upto > size {
            upto = size
        }
        if from - 1 >= upto {
            return ok(SqlValue.Text(""))
        }
        return ok(SqlValue.Text(substring(text, from - 1, upto)))
    }
    if name == "lower" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Text(to_lower(text_of(given[0]), invariant)))
    }
    if name == "upper" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Text(to_upper(text_of(given[0]), invariant)))
    }
    if name == "trim" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Text(trim(text_of(given[0]))))
    }
    if name == "abs" {
        val v = given[0]
        if is_int(v) {
            val n = unwrap_int(v)
            return ok(SqlValue.Int(if n < 0 { 0 - n } else { n }))
        }
        val f = as_number(v)
        return ok(SqlValue.Float(if f < 0.0 { 0.0 - f } else { f }))
    }
    // What is left is a known name with no scalar implementation, which is every aggregate: this
    // is reached when one is written where no group exists, such as in a `WHERE`. SQLite's words
    // again, and the right ones -- the function is not missing, it is in the wrong place.
    return err(problem(concat("misuse of aggregate function ", node.name, "()")))
}

fn eval_binary(node: BinNode, cx: Ctx) -> Result<SqlValue, DbError> {
    // AND and OR short-circuit on a decisive left side, which is also what keeps
    // `x IS NOT NULL AND x > 3` from reading a NULL as a number.
    if node.op == "AND" {
        val left = try eval_expr(node.left, cx)
        if !is_null(left) && !is_true(left) {
            return ok(SqlValue.Bool(false))
        }
        val right = try eval_expr(node.right, cx)
        if is_null(left) || is_null(right) {
            return if !is_null(right) && !is_true(right) { ok(SqlValue.Bool(false)) } else { ok(SqlValue.Null) }
        }
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(is_true(left) && is_true(right)))
    }
    if node.op == "OR" {
        val left = try eval_expr(node.left, cx)
        if is_true(left) {
            return ok(SqlValue.Bool(true))
        }
        val right = try eval_expr(node.right, cx)
        if is_true(right) {
            return ok(SqlValue.Bool(true))
        }
        return if is_null(left) || is_null(right) { ok(SqlValue.Null) } else { ok(SqlValue.Bool(false)) }
    }
    val left = try eval_expr(node.left, cx)
    val right = try eval_expr(node.right, cx)
    // Every other operator is NULL when either side is: comparing or adding an unknown gives an
    // unknown. This is the rule that makes `WHERE col = NULL` match nothing, which surprises
    // people and is correct.
    if is_null(left) || is_null(right) {
        return ok(SqlValue.Null)
    }
    if node.op == "||" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Text(concat(text_of(left), text_of(right))))
    }
    // Comparing a number with text is a type error in the standard and in PostgreSQL ("operator
    // does not exist: integer = text"); SQLite orders the two by a type rank instead, which is
    // how a query that silently matches nothing gets written. The strict connection refuses it.
    if cx.strict && type_rank(left) != type_rank(right) && is_comparison(node.op) {
        return err(problem(prelude.format(
            "cannot compare {} with {} (the strict engine refuses what SQLite would order by type)",
            sql_type_of(left),
            sql_type_of(right)
        )))
    }
    if node.op == "=" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(compare_values(left, right) == 0))
    }
    if node.op == "<>" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(compare_values(left, right) != 0))
    }
    if node.op == "<" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(compare_values(left, right) < 0))
    }
    if node.op == "<=" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(compare_values(left, right) <= 0))
    }
    if node.op == ">" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(compare_values(left, right) > 0))
    }
    if node.op == ">=" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(compare_values(left, right) >= 0))
    }
    // Arithmetic stays in integers when both sides are, so `3 / 2` is 1 as it is in SQLite and
    // PostgreSQL, and becomes a float as soon as either side is one.
    val both_int = is_int(left) && is_int(right)
    if node.op == "+" || node.op == "-" || node.op == "*" {
        val a = as_number(left)
        val b = as_number(right)
        val f = if node.op == "+" { a + b } else if node.op == "-" { a - b } else { a * b }
        return if both_int { ok(SqlValue.Int(f as int)) } else { ok(SqlValue.Float(f)) }
    }
    if node.op == "/" {
        val b = as_number(right)
        if b == 0.0 {
            // The standard makes this an error (SQLSTATE 22012), which PostgreSQL raises and
            // SQLite does not -- it answers NULL. The strict connection follows the standard.
            if cx.strict {
                return err(problem("division by zero"))
            }
            return ok(SqlValue.Null)
        }
        return if both_int {
            ok(SqlValue.Int(unwrap_int(left) / unwrap_int(right)))
        } else {
            ok(SqlValue.Float(as_number(left) / b))
        }
    }
    return err(problem(prelude.format("unsupported operator '{}'", node.op)))
}

// A value as `||` renders it, which is how both real engines read a number in a concatenation.
fn text_of(v: SqlValue) -> string {
    return switch v {
        Text(s) { s }
        Int(n) { str(n) }
        Float(f) { str(f) }
        Bool(b) { if b { "1" } else { "0" } }
        Null { "" }
        Bytes(_) { "" }
    }
}

// `CAST(x AS t)`, by the leading word of the declared type: SQLite decides by affinity and looks
// at the whole name, and these are the families both engines agree on. An unknown type is an error
// rather than a value passed through unchanged, because a cast that silently does nothing is worse
// than one that says it cannot.
fn cast_value(v: SqlValue, type_name: string, strict: bool) -> Result<SqlValue, DbError> {
    if is_null(v) {
        return ok(SqlValue.Null)
    }
    val head = sql.fold_name(trim(switch split_once(type_name, "(") {
        Some(parts) { parts[0] }
        None { type_name }
    }))
    if contains(head, "int") {
        return switch v {
            Text(t) { switch parse_int(trim(t)) {
                Ok(n) { ok(SqlValue.Int(n)) }
                // SQLite reads the leading digits and answers 0 for none; the standard raises.
                Err(_) { if strict { err(problem(concat(
                    "cannot cast to ",
                    type_name,
                    ": ",
                    t
                ))) } else { ok(SqlValue.Int(0)) } }
            } }
            Float(f) { ok(SqlValue.Int(f as int)) }
            Bool(b) { ok(SqlValue.Int(if b { 1 } else { 0 })) }
            Int(_) { ok(v) }
            Null { ok(SqlValue.Null) }
            Bytes(_) { err(problem("cannot cast a blob to an integer")) }
        }
    }
    if head == "real" || head == "float" || head == "double" || head == "numeric" || head == "decimal" {
        return switch v {
            Text(t) { switch parse_float(trim(t)) {
                Ok(f) { ok(SqlValue.Float(f)) }
                Err(_) { if strict { err(problem(concat(
                    "cannot cast to ",
                    type_name,
                    ": ",
                    t
                ))) } else { ok(SqlValue.Float(0.0)) } }
            } }
            Int(n) { ok(SqlValue.Float(n as float)) }
            Bool(b) { ok(SqlValue.Float(if b { 1.0 } else { 0.0 })) }
            Float(_) { ok(v) }
            Null { ok(SqlValue.Null) }
            Bytes(_) { err(problem("cannot cast a blob to a real")) }
        }
    }
    if head == "text" || head == "varchar" || head == "char" || head == "character" || head == "clob" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Text(text_of(v)))
    }
    if head == "boolean" || head == "bool" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Bool(is_true(v)))
    }
    if head == "blob" || head == "binary" || head == "varbinary" {
        return switch v {
            Bytes(_) { ok(v) }
            Text(t) { ok(SqlValue.Bytes(to_bytes(t))) }
            Int(_) { ok(SqlValue.Bytes(to_bytes(text_of(v)))) }
            Float(_) { ok(SqlValue.Bytes(to_bytes(text_of(v)))) }
            Bool(_) { ok(SqlValue.Bytes(to_bytes(text_of(v)))) }
            Null { ok(SqlValue.Null) }
        }
    }
    return err(problem(concat("cannot cast to an unknown type: ", type_name)))
}

fn is_comparison(op: string) -> bool {
    return op == "=" || op == "<>" || op == "<" || op == "<=" || op == ">" || op == ">="
}

fn sql_type_of(v: SqlValue) -> string {
    return switch v {
        Null { "NULL" }
        Int(_) { "INTEGER" }
        Float(_) { "REAL" }
        Text(_) { "TEXT" }
        Bool(_) { "BOOLEAN" }
        Bytes(_) { "BLOB" }
    }
}

fn is_int(v: SqlValue) -> bool {
    return switch v {
        Int(_) { true }
        Bool(_) { true }
        Null { false }
        Float(_) { false }
        Text(_) { false }
        Bytes(_) { false }
    }
}

fn unwrap_int(v: SqlValue) -> int {
    return switch v {
        Int(n) { n }
        Bool(b) { if b { 1 } else { 0 } }
        Float(f) { f as int }
        Null { 0 }
        Text(_) { 0 }
        Bytes(_) { 0 }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- constraints

// Every constraint a table carries, gathered into one list: the table-level ones, each column's
// own, and a `PrimaryKey`/`Unique` for every column that was flagged singly. Walking one list is
// what keeps `INSERT` and `UPDATE` from enforcing different sets.
fn all_constraints(columns: [Column], table_level: [TableConstraint]) -> [TableConstraint] {
    val out = mutable_array_new<TableConstraint>()
    for col in columns {
        if col.primary_key {
            push(out, TableConstraint {
                name: "",
                rule: ConstraintRule.PrimaryKey([col.name]),
                text: "PRIMARY KEY"
            })
        }
        if col.unique {
            push(out, TableConstraint { name: "", rule: ConstraintRule.Unique([col.name]), text: "UNIQUE" })
        }
        for tc in col.constraints {
            push(out, tc)
        }
    }
    for tc in table_level {
        push(out, tc)
    }
    return snapshot(out)
}

// What SQLite quotes when a `CHECK` fails: the constraint's name where it has one, and otherwise
// **the expression as it was written**, brackets stripped. Taken from the source text rather than
// re-formatted, so `CHECK (a<b)` is quoted as `a<b` the way SQLite quotes it.
fn check_label(tc: TableConstraint) -> string {
    if tc.name != "" {
        return tc.name
    }
    val open = index_of(tc.text, "(", 0)
    val close = last_index_of(tc.text, ")")
    if open >= 0 && close > open {
        return trim(substring(tc.text, open + 1, close))
    }
    return tc.text
}

// `t.a, t.b` -- how SQLite names the columns of a failed `UNIQUE` or `PRIMARY KEY`.
fn qualified_columns(t: Table, columns: [string]) -> string {
    val out = mutable_array_new<string>()
    for name in columns {
        push(out, concat(t.name, ".", name))
    }
    return join(separator: ", ", pieces: snapshot(out))
}

// The index of a single-column `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY`, or -1.
//
// **That column is SQLite's rowid under another name**, so a NULL in it -- or leaving it out of the
// `INSERT` -- takes the next value instead of being refused. The declared type must be exactly
// `INTEGER`: measured, not guessed, `INT PRIMARY KEY` and `BIGINT PRIMARY KEY` are ordinary columns
// there and keep the NULL. PostgreSQL has no rowid, so the strict reading does none of this and
// refuses a NULL in a key instead.
fn rowid_column(t: Table) -> int {
    var found = 0 - 1
    var keys = 0
    for i in 0..prelude.len(t.columns) {
        if t.columns[i].primary_key {
            keys = keys + 1
            if sql.fold_name(t.columns[i].type_name) == "integer" {
                found = i
            }
        }
    }
    // A composite primary key is not a rowid, even if one of its columns is an INTEGER.
    if keys != 1 {
        return 0 - 1
    }
    return found
}

fn next_rowid(rows: [[SqlValue]], at: int) -> SqlValue {
    var top = 0
    for row in rows {
        if !is_null(row[at]) && is_int(row[at]) {
            val n = unwrap_int(row[at])
            if n > top {
                top = n
            }
        }
    }
    return SqlValue.Int(top + 1)
}

// Every constraint on `t` checked against the rows it would hold, where `touched` names the rows
// this statement wrote. Row-level rules are checked on those rows only; uniqueness compares them
// against the whole table, which is why both are here rather than in two passes over the data.
//
// **Checked before the new table is published**, so a statement that violates a constraint leaves
// the database exactly as it was -- the `Database` being a value is what makes that free.
fn enforce(database: Database, t: Table, touched: [int], params: [SqlValue], strict: bool) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    for i in touched {
        val row = t.rows[i]
        for k in 0..prelude.len(t.columns) {
            if t.columns[k].not_null && is_null(row[k]) {
                return err(DbError.Constraint(prelude.format(
                    "NOT NULL constraint failed: {}.{}",
                    t.name,
                    t.columns[k].name
                )))
            }
        }
    }
    for tc in t.constraints {
        for i in touched {
            try one_constraint(database, t, tc, i, params, strict)
        }
    }
    return ok(unit)
}

fn one_constraint(
    database: Database,
    t: Table,
    tc: TableConstraint,
    at: int,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool
) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    val row = t.rows[at]
    val names = column_names(t)
    return switch tc.rule {
        PrimaryKey(columns) {
            // **SQLite lets a NULL sit in a primary key**; the standard and PostgreSQL make a key
            // implicitly `NOT NULL`. The rowid case never reaches here, having been filled in.
            if strict {
                for name in columns {
                    val k = column_index(names, name)
                    if k >= 0 && is_null(row[k]) {
                        return err(DbError.Constraint(prelude.format(
                            "a PRIMARY KEY column may not be NULL: {}.{} (the standard and PostgreSQL refuse what SQLite allows)",
                            t.name,
                            name
                        )))
                    }
                }
            }
            unique_check(t, tc, columns, at)
        }
        Unique(columns) { unique_check(t, tc, columns, at) }
        Check(e) {
            val v = try eval_expr(e, table_ctx(database, t, row, params, strict))
            // A `CHECK` fails only when it is *false*: NULL is unknown and passes, in both engines
            // and in the standard.
            if !is_null(v) && !is_true(v) {
                return err(DbError.Constraint(concat("CHECK constraint failed: ", check_label(tc))))
            }
            unit
        }
        // Referential integrity is the next increment; the rule is modelled and not yet enforced.
        ForeignKey(_) { unit }
        Opaque { unit }
    }
}

// A key's uniqueness. **A key with a NULL in it conflicts with nothing**, which is the standard's
// rule and both engines': `UNIQUE` asks whether two keys are *equal*, and a NULL is never equal to
// anything -- the opposite reading of NULL from the one `DISTINCT` and `GROUP BY` take.
fn unique_check(t: Table, tc: TableConstraint, columns: [string], at: int) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    val names = column_names(t)
    val places = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for name in columns {
        val k = column_index(names, name)
        if k < 0 {
            return err(problem(prelude.format("no such column: {} in a constraint on {}", name, t.name)))
        }
        push(places, k)
    }
    val where_at = snapshot(places)
    val key = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
    for k in where_at {
        if is_null(t.rows[at][k]) {
            return ok(unit)
        }
        push(key, t.rows[at][k])
    }
    val wanted = snapshot(key)
    for i in 0..prelude.len(t.rows) {
        if i == at {
            continue
        }
        val other = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
        var null_seen = false
        for k in where_at {
            if is_null(t.rows[i][k]) {
                null_seen = true
            }
            push(other, t.rows[i][k])
        }
        if !null_seen && values_same(snapshot(other), wanted) {
            return err(DbError.Constraint(concat(
                "UNIQUE constraint failed: ",
                qualified_columns(t, columns)
            )))
        }
    }
    return ok(unit)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- functions

// Every function this engine has, and how many arguments it takes -- `most` of -1 meaning any
// number.
//
// **One list, read by validation before a statement runs and by evaluation as it runs.** The two
// being separate is what let three things through at once: `SELECT nosuchfunc(a) FROM empty`
// answered no rows where both real engines refuse the statement, because nothing ever evaluated
// the call; `GROUP BY ROLLUP (a)` was accepted for the same reason, `ROLLUP` parsing as an ordinary
// function call; and `SELECT upper()` **crashed the interpreter**, because the three functions with
// no arity check of their own read `args[0]` of an empty array. A function's arity is now a fact
// about the function, in one place, instead of a check each implementation remembers or forgets.
struct FnSpec {
    name: string
    least: int
    most: int
}

// `coalesce` takes two or more, which is SQLite's rule as well -- `coalesce(x)` is refused there,
// not read as `x`. The aggregates take exactly one argument, `count(*)`'s star included.
val known_functions: [FnSpec] = [
    FnSpec { name: "coalesce", least: 2, most: 0 - 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "length", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "substr", least: 2, most: 3 },
    FnSpec { name: "substring", least: 2, most: 3 },
    FnSpec { name: "lower", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "upper", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "trim", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "abs", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "count", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "sum", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "min", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "max", least: 1, most: 1 },
    FnSpec { name: "avg", least: 1, most: 1 }
]

// A call checked against that list: the name must be one this engine has, and the count of
// arguments must be one the function takes. Both messages are SQLite's, which is what lets the
// cross-check fixture report agreement rather than a near miss -- and the name is echoed as it was
// written, as SQLite echoes it.
fn check_call(node: CallNode) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    val want = sql.fold_name(node.name)
    for spec in known_functions {
        if spec.name == want {
            val given = prelude.len(node.args)
            if given < spec.least || (spec.most >= 0 && given > spec.most) {
                return err(problem(concat("wrong number of arguments to function ", node.name, "()")))
            }
            return ok(unit)
        }
    }
    return err(problem(concat("no such function: ", node.name)))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- aggregates

// The columns a `FROM` produces, with no rows read: names and qualifiers only. Validation needs
// exactly this, and getting it from the table definitions rather than from a built source means a
// mistyped column is refused before a join is computed.
fn shape_ctx(
    database: Database,
    stmt: SelectStmt,
    outer: [Ctx],
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool
) -> Result<Ctx, DbError> {
    val columns = mutable_array_new<string>()
    val tables = mutable_array_new<string>()
    val refs = mutable_array_new<sql.TableRef>()
    for r in stmt.from {
        push(refs, r)
    }
    for j in stmt.joins {
        push(refs, j.table)
    }
    for r in refs {
        val t = try table_of(database, r.name)
        val qualifier = if r.alias == "" { t.name } else { r.alias }
        for name in column_names(t) {
            push(columns, name)
            push(tables, qualifier)
        }
    }
    val no_row: [SqlValue] = []
    var cx = ctx_of(database, snapshot(columns), snapshot(tables), no_row, params, strict)
    for o in outer {
        cx = nested_in(cx, o)
    }
    return ok(cx)
}

// Every expression of a `SELECT`, checked against the shape its `FROM` produces.
//
// `ORDER BY` is checked against that shape *plus* the names the select list gives its columns,
// because `ORDER BY alias` is legal SQL and naming an output column there is not a mistake.
fn validate_query(stmt: SelectStmt, outer_cx: Ctx) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    val outer = push(outer_cx.outer, outer_cx)
    return validate_select(outer_cx.database, stmt, outer, outer_cx.params, outer_cx.strict)
}

fn validate_select(
    database: Database,
    stmt: SelectStmt,
    outer: [Ctx],
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool
) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    val cx = try shape_ctx(database, stmt, outer, params, strict)
    for i in 0..prelude.len(stmt.items) {
        try validate_expr(stmt.items[i].expr, cx)
    }
    if Some(e) = stmt.filter {
        try validate_expr(e, cx)
    }
    for g in stmt.groups {
        try validate_expr(g, cx)
    }
    if Some(e) = stmt.having {
        try validate_expr(e, cx)
    }
    for term in stmt.order {
        // `ORDER BY` resolves against the source first and against the select list's own names
        // second, which is the order SQL gives it -- a `name` that is both a column and an alias
        // is the column, not an ambiguity.
        if Err(e) = validate_expr(term.expr, cx) {
            if !names_an_output(term.expr, stmt) {
                return err(e)
            }
        }
    }
    return ok(unit)
}

// Whether an `ORDER BY` term is simply the name the select list gave one of its columns.
fn names_an_output(e: Expr, stmt: SelectStmt) -> bool {
    if Col(ref) = e {
        if ref.table == "" {
            for i in 0..prelude.len(stmt.items) {
                if sql.fold_name(item_name(stmt.items[i], i)) == sql.fold_name(ref.name) {
                    return true
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return false
}

// Every column an expression names, checked against the table before anything runs.
//
// **A real engine resolves columns when it prepares a statement, and this one looked them up as it
// read each row** -- so a query whose `WHERE` matched nothing never looked, and
// `SELECT count(age) FROM visit WHERE reason = 'nothing'` answered 0 where SQLite answers "no such
// column: age". A typo in a column name passing on `memdb` and failing on the real engine is the
// precise false green this whole arrangement exists to prevent, and the cross-check fixture caught
// it on its first run against the aggregates.
// Whether `ref` names a column of this scope or of one enclosing it. The same walk
// `lookup_column` makes, without a row to read -- which is the point: validation happens before
// any row is in hand, and a correlated subquery's name may belong to an outer query.
fn column_known(cx: Ctx, ref: sql.ColRef) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    val at = resolve_column(cx, ref)
    if at == 0 - 2 {
        return err(problem(concat("ambiguous column name: ", ref.name)))
    }
    if at >= 0 {
        return ok(unit)
    }
    val depth = prelude.len(cx.outer)
    if depth > 0 {
        return column_known(cx.outer[depth - 1], ref)
    }
    val written = if ref.table == "" { ref.name } else { concat(ref.table, ".", ref.name) }
    return err(problem(concat("no such column: ", written)))
}

// Whether `table` names a table this scope reads, by its alias where one was given. The same
// outward walk `column_known` makes, because `t.*` inside a correlated subquery may name a table
// of the enclosing query.
fn table_known(cx: Ctx, table: string) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    val want = sql.fold_name(table)
    for name in cx.tables {
        if sql.fold_name(name) == want {
            return ok(unit)
        }
    }
    val depth = prelude.len(cx.outer)
    if depth > 0 {
        return table_known(cx.outer[depth - 1], table)
    }
    return err(problem(concat("no such table: ", table)))
}

fn validate_expr(e: Expr, cx: Ctx) -> Result<unit, DbError> {
    return switch e {
        Col(ref) { column_known(cx, ref) }
        Not(node) { validate_expr(node.inner, cx) }
        IsNull(node) { validate_expr(node.inner, cx) }
        Bin(node) {
            try validate_expr(node.left, cx)
            validate_expr(node.right, cx)
        }
        Like(node) {
            try validate_expr(node.subject, cx)
            validate_expr(node.pattern, cx)
        }
        Between(node) {
            try validate_expr(node.subject, cx)
            try validate_expr(node.low, cx)
            validate_expr(node.high, cx)
        }
        In(node) {
            try validate_expr(node.subject, cx)
            for o in node.options {
                try validate_expr(o, cx)
            }
            unit
        }
        Case(node) {
            for arm in node.arms {
                try validate_expr(arm.condition, cx)
                try validate_expr(arm.value, cx)
            }
            for f in node.fallback {
                try validate_expr(f, cx)
            }
            unit
        }
        Call(node) {
            try check_call(node)
            for a in node.args {
                try validate_expr(a, cx)
            }
            unit
        }
        Lit(_) { unit }
        Param(_) { unit }
        Star { unit }
        TableStar(table) { table_known(cx, table) }
        // A subquery's own columns are checked against its own tables, with this scope enclosing
        // them -- that is what makes a correlated name legal and a mistyped one an error.
        Sub(node) { validate_query(node.query, cx) }
        Exists(node) { validate_query(node.query, cx) }
        InSub(node) {
            try validate_expr(node.subject, cx)
            validate_query(node.query, cx)
        }
        Cast(node) { validate_expr(node.inner, cx) }
    }
}

// A group's representative row, or no row at all for an empty group. An empty array literal
// needs its element type somewhere, and a function signature is the tidiest place to put it.
// A value on its way out to the caller.
//
// **SQLite has no boolean type**, so a comparison in a select list comes back as the integer 0 or
// 1; PostgreSQL and the standard have a real boolean and answer one. That makes it the fourth place
// the two readings part, and the lax one follows SQLite -- which is also why `db.sql_as_bool`
// accepts an `Int` of 0 or 1 in the first place.
//
// Only the projection is converted. Inside a `WHERE` or a `HAVING` a boolean stays a boolean,
// because nothing outside sees it.
fn present(v: SqlValue, strict: bool) -> SqlValue {
    if strict {
        return v
    }
    return switch v {
        Bool(b) { SqlValue.Int(if b { 1 } else { 0 }) }
        Null { v }
        Int(_) { v }
        Float(_) { v }
        Text(_) { v }
        Bytes(_) { v }
    }
}

fn first_row(rows: [[SqlValue]]) -> [SqlValue] {
    if prelude.len(rows) > 0 {
        return rows[0]
    }
    val none: [SqlValue] = []
    return none
}

fn is_aggregate(name: string) -> bool {
    val n = sql.fold_name(name)
    return n == "count" || n == "sum" || n == "min" || n == "max" || n == "avg"
}

// Whether an expression contains an aggregate anywhere, which is what decides that a `SELECT` with
// no `GROUP BY` is nonetheless one group over every matching row.
fn has_aggregate(e: Expr) -> bool {
    return switch e {
        Call(node) {
            if is_aggregate(node.name) {
                true
            } else {
                var found = false
                for a in node.args {
                    if has_aggregate(a) {
                        found = true
                    }
                }
                found
            }
        }
        Not(node) { has_aggregate(node.inner) }
        IsNull(node) { has_aggregate(node.inner) }
        Bin(node) { has_aggregate(node.left) || has_aggregate(node.right) }
        Like(node) { has_aggregate(node.subject) || has_aggregate(node.pattern) }
        Between(node) { has_aggregate(node.subject) || has_aggregate(node.low) || has_aggregate(node.high) }
        In(node) {
            var found = has_aggregate(node.subject)
            for o in node.options {
                if has_aggregate(o) {
                    found = true
                }
            }
            found
        }
        Case(node) {
            var found = false
            for arm in node.arms {
                if has_aggregate(arm.condition) || has_aggregate(arm.value) {
                    found = true
                }
            }
            for f in node.fallback {
                if has_aggregate(f) {
                    found = true
                }
            }
            found
        }
        Lit(_) { false }
        Col(_) { false }
        Param(_) { false }
        Star { false }
        TableStar(_) { false }
        // An aggregate inside a subquery belongs to that subquery, not to this one.
        Sub(_) { false }
        Exists(_) { false }
        InSub(node) { has_aggregate(node.subject) }
        Cast(node) { has_aggregate(node.inner) }
    }
}

// One aggregate over one group, by the standard's rules -- which both real engines follow, and
// which are the ones a reader guesses wrong:
//
//   - `COUNT(*)` counts **rows**, including a row whose every column is NULL;
//   - `COUNT(expr)` counts the rows where `expr` is **not** NULL;
//   - every other aggregate **ignores** NULLs rather than propagating them, unlike every scalar
//     operator in SQL;
//   - `SUM`, `MIN`, `MAX` and `AVG` over no surviving value are **NULL**, while `COUNT` is **0**.
//     That asymmetry is the one worth having written down: `SELECT SUM(x) FROM empty` is NULL and
//     `SELECT COUNT(x) FROM empty` is 0.
//
// `AVG` answers a float, as SQLite does; PostgreSQL answers `numeric` for an integer column, and
// the standard leaves the exact-numeric result type implementation-defined.
fn eval_aggregate(node: CallNode, sc: Scope, rows: [[SqlValue]]) -> Result<SqlValue, DbError> {
    try check_call(node)
    val name = sql.fold_name(node.name)
    val arg = node.args[0]
    if name == "count" && Expr.is_Star(arg) {
        return ok(SqlValue.Int(prelude.len(rows)))
    }
    val kept = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
    for row in rows {
        val v = try eval_expr(arg, scope_ctx(sc, row))
        if !is_null(v) {
            push(kept, v)
        }
    }
    var values = snapshot(kept)
    // `COUNT(DISTINCT x)`, and the same for the others: duplicates are dropped before the
    // aggregate sees them. NULLs are already gone, so distinctness here is plain comparison.
    if node.distinct {
        val once = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
        for v in values {
            var seen = false
            for already in once {
                if !seen && compare_values(v, already) == 0 {
                    seen = true
                }
            }
            if !seen {
                push(once, v)
            }
        }
        values = snapshot(once)
    }
    if name == "count" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Int(prelude.len(values)))
    }
    if prelude.len(values) == 0 {
        return ok(SqlValue.Null)
    }
    if name == "min" || name == "max" {
        var best = values[0]
        for v in values {
            val c = compare_values(v, best)
            if (name == "min" && c < 0) || (name == "max" && c > 0) {
                best = v
            }
        }
        return ok(best)
    }
    var total = 0.0
    var all_int = true
    for v in values {
        total = total + as_number(v)
        if !is_int(v) {
            all_int = false
        }
    }
    if name == "avg" {
        return ok(SqlValue.Float(total / (prelude.len(values) as float)))
    }
    return if all_int { ok(SqlValue.Int(total as int)) } else { ok(SqlValue.Float(total)) }
}

// An expression evaluated for a whole group: an aggregate call folds the group's rows, and
// anything else is read from the group's first row.
//
// **Reading a non-grouped column is allowed here**, as SQLite allows it, where the standard and
// PostgreSQL require every bare column to be a grouping column. It is a laxness rather than a
// difference in answers for the queries this engine is for, and the strict connection does not
// tighten it -- that would need the parser to know which columns are grouped, which is the next
// increment's problem rather than this one's.
fn eval_grouped(e: Expr, sc: Scope, rows: [[SqlValue]]) -> Result<SqlValue, DbError> {
    if Call(node) = e {
        if is_aggregate(node.name) {
            return eval_aggregate(node, sc, rows)
        }
    }
    if has_aggregate(e) {
        // An aggregate nested inside an expression: fold each piece for the group, then combine.
        return switch e {
            Not(node) {
                val inner = try eval_grouped(node.inner, sc, rows)
                if is_null(inner) { ok(SqlValue.Null) } else { ok(SqlValue.Bool(!is_true(inner))) }
            }
            IsNull(node) {
                val inner = try eval_grouped(node.inner, sc, rows)
                ok(SqlValue.Bool(if node.negated { !is_null(inner) } else { is_null(inner) }))
            }
            Bin(node) {
                val left = try eval_grouped(node.left, sc, rows)
                val right = try eval_grouped(node.right, sc, rows)
                // Folded to literals, then run through the ordinary binary evaluator so the NULL
                // rules and the strict checks are written once.
                eval_binary(
                    BinNode { op: node.op, left: Expr.Lit(left), right: Expr.Lit(right) },
                    scope_ctx(sc, first_row(rows))
                )
            }
            Case(node) {
                var answer = SqlValue.Null
                var decided = false
                for arm in node.arms {
                    if !decided {
                        val test = try eval_grouped(arm.condition, sc, rows)
                        if is_true(test) {
                            answer = try eval_grouped(arm.value, sc, rows)
                            decided = true
                        }
                    }
                }
                if !decided && prelude.len(node.fallback) == 1 {
                    answer = try eval_grouped(node.fallback[0], sc, rows)
                }
                ok(answer)
            }
            Call(node) {
                val args = mutable_array_new<Expr>()
                for a in node.args {
                    push(args, Expr.Lit(try eval_grouped(a, sc, rows)))
                }
                eval_call(
                    CallNode { name: node.name, args: snapshot(args), distinct: node.distinct },
                    scope_ctx(sc, first_row(rows))
                )
            }
            Lit(v) { ok(v) }
            Col(_) { err(problem("unreachable: a column holds no aggregate")) }
            Param(_) { err(problem("unreachable: a parameter holds no aggregate")) }
            Star { err(problem("'*' is only allowed inside COUNT")) }
            TableStar(_) { err(problem("'*' is only allowed inside COUNT")) }
            Like(_) { err(problem("an aggregate inside LIKE is not supported")) }
            In(_) { err(problem("an aggregate inside IN is not supported")) }
            Between(_) { err(problem("an aggregate inside BETWEEN is not supported")) }
            InSub(_) { err(problem("an aggregate inside IN (SELECT ..) is not supported")) }
            Sub(_) { err(problem("unreachable: a subquery holds no aggregate of this query")) }
            Exists(_) { err(problem("unreachable: EXISTS holds no aggregate of this query")) }
            Cast(node) {
                val inner = try eval_grouped(node.inner, sc, rows)
                cast_value(inner, node.type_name, sc.strict)
            }
        }
    }
    return eval_expr(e, scope_ctx(sc, first_row(rows)))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- running statements

// What a statement did: the database after it, the rows it answered and how many it changed.
struct Outcome {
    database: Database
    rows: [Row]
    affected: int
}

fn table_of(database: Database, name: string) -> Result<Table, DbError> {
    return switch get(database.tables, sql.fold_name(name)) {
        Some(t) { ok(t) }
        None { err(problem(concat("no such table: ", name))) }
    }
}

fn with_table(database: Database, t: Table) -> Database {
    return Database { tables: set(database.tables, sql.fold_name(t.name), t) }
}

fn column_names(t: Table) -> [string] {
    val out = mutable_array_new<string>()
    for c in t.columns {
        push(out, c.name)
    }
    return snapshot(out)
}

// The rows of `t` that `filter` keeps, as indexes into `t.rows` so an UPDATE or DELETE can work on
// positions rather than copies.
fn matching(
    database: Database,
    t: Table,
    filter: Option<Expr>,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool
) -> Result<[int], DbError> {
    val names = column_names(t)
    val out = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for i in 0..prelude.len(t.rows) {
        var keep = true
        if Some(e) = filter {
            keep = is_true(try eval_expr(e, table_ctx(database, t, t.rows[i], params, strict)))
        }
        if keep {
            push(out, i)
        }
    }
    return ok(snapshot(out))
}

fn run_create(database: Database, stmt: CreateStmt) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    if contains(database.tables, sql.fold_name(stmt.table)) {
        if stmt.if_not_exists {
            return ok(Outcome { database: database, rows: [], affected: 0 })
        }
        return err(problem(concat("table already exists: ", stmt.table)))
    }
    val t = Table {
        name: stmt.table,
        columns: stmt.columns,
        rows: [],
        constraints: all_constraints(stmt.columns, stmt.constraints)
    }
    return ok(Outcome { database: with_table(database, t), rows: [], affected: 0 })
}

// `ALTER TABLE ... ADD` -- a column, or a table constraint.
//
// The constraint form is **refused by the SQLite-shaped reading**, in SQLite's own words, because
// SQLite has no `ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT` at all; PostgreSQL and the standard have it. The
// strict reading accepts it and **records it nowhere**, which is the one place this engine still
// takes a constraint without keeping it: enforcing a constraint added after the fact means
// checking the rows already there against it, and that is referential integrity's increment to
// bring. Saying so beats pretending.
fn run_alter(database: Database, stmt: AlterStmt, strict: bool) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val t = try table_of(database, stmt.table)
    val column = switch stmt.action {
        AddColumn(col) { col }
        AddConstraint(text) {
            if !strict {
                // SQLite names the first word of what it could not read, and so does this.
                val space = index_of(text, " ", 0)
                val first = if space < 0 { text } else { substring(text, 0, space) }
                return err(problem(prelude.format("near \"{}\": syntax error", first)))
            }
            return ok(Outcome { database: database, rows: [], affected: 0 })
        }
    }
    if column_index(column_names(t), column.name) >= 0 {
        return err(problem(prelude.format("column {} already exists on {}", column.name, t.name)))
    }
    // SQLite refuses both of these outright, in these words, because it adds a column without
    // rewriting the rows that are already there. PostgreSQL rewrites, so it refuses the first only
    // when the table has rows to falsify it and accepts the second always; the strict reading
    // follows PostgreSQL.
    if column.not_null && Option.is_None(column.default_value) {
        if !strict || prelude.len(t.rows) > 0 {
            return err(problem("Cannot add a NOT NULL column with default value NULL"))
        }
    }
    if column.unique && !strict {
        return err(problem("Cannot add a UNIQUE column"))
    }
    // Existing rows gain the new column's default, or NULL -- which is what the real engines do
    // for an added column, and why `NOT NULL` without a default is refused there and merely
    // recorded here.
    val filler = switch column.default_value {
        Some(v) { v }
        None { SqlValue.Null }
    }
    val rows = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for row in t.rows {
        push(rows, push(row, filler))
    }
    val grown = with t {
        columns: push(t.columns, column),
        rows: snapshot(rows),
        // The added column brings its own flags and constraints with it.
        constraints: concat(t.constraints, all_constraints([column], []))
    }
    return ok(Outcome { database: with_table(database, grown), rows: [], affected: 0 })
}

fn run_drop(database: Database, stmt: DropStmt) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    if !contains(database.tables, sql.fold_name(stmt.table)) {
        if stmt.if_exists {
            return ok(Outcome { database: database, rows: [], affected: 0 })
        }
        return err(problem(concat("no such table: ", stmt.table)))
    }
    return ok(Outcome {
        database: Database { tables: remove(database.tables, sql.fold_name(stmt.table)) },
        rows: [],
        affected: 0
    })
}

// The rows a `RETURNING` clause answers: its items projected over the rows the write touched.
//
// Which rows those are differs per statement and is the caller's business -- an `INSERT` returns
// what it stored (defaults filled in), an `UPDATE` the row as it now stands, a `DELETE` the row as
// it was. Each is the one a real engine returns.
//
// **Not ANSI SQL**, and deliberately not gated on the strict reading. The standard's form is a data
// change delta table (`SELECT * FROM FINAL TABLE (INSERT ..)`, SQL:2011), which essentially only
// Db2 implements; `RETURNING` is PostgreSQL's, SQLite has it from 3.35 and MySQL has nothing of the
// kind. So it is the *dialect* that answers whether a connection has it, not `strict` -- and the
// sample application already asks exactly that way, `datasource.dialect(conn)` against a list of
// engines. Refusing it under `memdb-ansi:` would break every write in that application on a flag
// whose job is to find SQL that leans on SQLite, which this does not.
fn returned_rows(
    database: Database,
    t: Table,
    items: [SelectItem],
    touched: [[SqlValue]],
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool
) -> Result<[Row], DbError> {
    val empty: [Row] = []
    if prelude.len(items) == 0 {
        return ok(empty)
    }
    val names = column_names(t)
    val shape = table_ctx(database, t, [], params, strict)
    for item in items {
        // **`RETURNING t.*` is where the two readings part, and in the opposite direction to every
        // other divergence**: SQLite refuses a qualified star in a `RETURNING` clause outright,
        // and PostgreSQL answers it (measured on postgres:17, not assumed). So the SQLite-shaped
        // reading refuses it, with SQLite's own words, and the other one runs it. A statement that
        // fails on `memdb:` and passes on `memdb-ansi:` is the mirror of the usual signal: it is
        // PostgreSQL-only SQL, and it will break on SQLite.
        if !strict && Expr.is_TableStar(item.expr) {
            return err(problem("RETURNING may not use \"TABLE.*\" wildcards"))
        }
        try validate_expr(item.expr, shape)
    }
    // `RETURNING *` answers every column of the table, by the same rule a select list's `*`
    // follows -- including beside other items, as `RETURNING *, id`.
    val columns = try item_headers(items, names, shape.tables)
    val out = mutable_array_new<Row>()
    for row in touched {
        val values = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
        for item in items {
            if is_star_item(item.expr) {
                for at in try star_positions(item.expr, names, shape.tables) {
                    push(values, present(row[at], strict))
                }
            } else {
                push(values, present(
                    try eval_expr(item.expr, table_ctx(database, t, row, params, strict)),
                    strict
                ))
            }
        }
        push(out, Row { columns: columns, values: snapshot(values) })
    }
    return ok(snapshot(out))
}

fn run_insert(database: Database, stmt: InsertStmt, params: [SqlValue], strict: bool) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val t = try table_of(database, stmt.table)
    val names = column_names(t)
    // With no column list the values are positional, in declaration order.
    val targets = if prelude.len(stmt.columns) > 0 { stmt.columns } else { names }
    val positions = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for name in targets {
        val at = column_index(names, name)
        if at < 0 {
            return err(problem(prelude.format("no such column: {} on {}", name, t.name)))
        }
        push(positions, at)
    }
    val where_to = snapshot(positions)
    val rowid = if strict { 0 - 1 } else { rowid_column(t) }
    var rows = t.rows
    var added = 0
    val stored = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    val touched = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for values in stmt.rows {
        if prelude.len(values) != prelude.len(where_to) {
            return err(problem(prelude.format(
                "{} values for {} columns",
                prelude.len(values),
                prelude.len(where_to)
            )))
        }
        // Start from each column's default (NULL where there is none), then place the values.
        val row = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
        for c in t.columns {
            push(row, switch c.default_value {
                Some(v) { v }
                None { SqlValue.Null }
            })
        }
        for i in 0..prelude.len(where_to) {
            // An INSERT's expressions see no row, so a column reference in one is an error rather
            // than a silent NULL.
            row[where_to[i]] = try eval_expr(values[i], bare_ctx(database, params, strict))
        }
        // A single-column `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` left NULL takes the next value, as SQLite's rowid
        // does. Done before the constraints are checked, so the NOT NULL on a key never sees the
        // NULL that was about to be replaced -- which is also how SQLite reads
        // `a INTEGER PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL`.
        if rowid >= 0 && is_null(row[rowid]) {
            row[rowid] = next_rowid(rows, rowid)
        }
        val written = snapshot(row)
        push(touched, prelude.len(rows))
        rows = push(rows, written)
        push(stored, written)
        added = added + 1
    }
    val filled = with t { rows: rows }
    try enforce(database, filled, snapshot(touched), params, strict)
    return ok(Outcome {
        database: with_table(database, filled),
        rows: try returned_rows(database, filled, stmt.returning, snapshot(stored), params, strict),
        affected: added
    })
}

fn run_delete(database: Database, stmt: DeleteStmt, params: [SqlValue], strict: bool) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val t = try table_of(database, stmt.table)
    if Some(e) = stmt.filter {
        try validate_expr(e, table_ctx(database, t, [], params, strict))
    }
    val doomed = to_set(try matching(database, t, stmt.filter, params, strict))
    val rows = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    val gone = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for i in 0..prelude.len(t.rows) {
        if contains(doomed, i) {
            push(gone, t.rows[i])
        } else {
            push(rows, t.rows[i])
        }
    }
    val left = with t { rows: snapshot(rows) }
    return ok(Outcome {
        database: with_table(database, left),
        // The row as it was, which is the only version of a deleted row there is.
        rows: try returned_rows(database, t, stmt.returning, snapshot(gone), params, strict),
        affected: prelude.len(doomed)
    })
}

fn run_update(database: Database, stmt: UpdateStmt, params: [SqlValue], strict: bool) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val t = try table_of(database, stmt.table)
    val names = column_names(t)
    val shape = table_ctx(database, t, [], params, strict)
    if Some(e) = stmt.filter {
        try validate_expr(e, shape)
    }
    for assignment in stmt.sets {
        try validate_expr(assignment.value, shape)
    }
    val touched = try matching(database, t, stmt.filter, params, strict)
    val chosen = to_set(touched)
    val rows = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    val written = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for i in 0..prelude.len(t.rows) {
        if !contains(chosen, i) {
            push(rows, t.rows[i])
            continue
        }
        var row = t.rows[i]
        for assignment in stmt.sets {
            val at = column_index(names, assignment.column)
            if at < 0 {
                return err(problem(prelude.format("no such column: {} on {}", assignment.column, t.name)))
            }
            // Every assignment of one statement reads the row as it was before the statement,
            // which is what `SET a = b, b = a` means in SQL.
            row = set(row, at, try eval_expr(assignment.value, table_ctx(database, t, t.rows[i], params, strict)))
        }
        push(rows, row)
        push(written, row)
    }
    val changed = with t { rows: snapshot(rows) }
    // The row indexes are unchanged -- one row goes back for every row that came out -- so the
    // rows this statement wrote are the ones it matched.
    try enforce(database, changed, touched, params, strict)
    return ok(Outcome {
        database: with_table(database, changed),
        // The row as it now stands, which is what both engines return and what a caller asking for
        // `RETURNING *` after an `UPDATE` is after.
        rows: try returned_rows(database, changed, stmt.returning, snapshot(written), params, strict),
        affected: prelude.len(chosen)
    })
}

// The output column name for a select item: its alias, else the column it names, else its
// position -- which is roughly what SQLite does and close enough for a test to read by name.
// Whether an item is a star of either kind, and so stands for columns rather than for a value.
fn is_star_item(e: Expr) -> bool {
    return Expr.is_Star(e) || Expr.is_TableStar(e)
}

// The column positions a star stands for: every column for a bare `*`, and one table's columns for
// `t.*`, matched against the qualifier each column was given by the `FROM` -- an alias where the
// table had one, which is what makes `SELECT p.* FROM patient AS p` work and `patient.*` not.
//
// A qualifier naming no table in scope is an error rather than an empty expansion, because
// `SELECT x.* FROM t` is a mistake and answering no columns would hide it. Real engines say
// "no such table: x"; so does this.
fn star_positions(e: Expr, names: [string], tables: [string]) -> Result<[int], DbError> {
    var qualifier = ""
    if TableStar(table) = e {
        qualifier = table
    }
    val want = sql.fold_name(qualifier)
    val out = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for i in 0..prelude.len(names) {
        if qualifier == "" || sql.fold_name(tables[i]) == want {
            push(out, i)
        }
    }
    if qualifier != "" && prelude.len(out) == 0 {
        return err(problem(concat("no such table: ", qualifier)))
    }
    return ok(snapshot(out))
}

// The output column names a list of items produces, with every star expanded. One function for a
// select list and for a `RETURNING` clause, which answer the same question.
fn item_headers(items: [SelectItem], names: [string], tables: [string]) -> Result<[string], DbError> {
    val headers = mutable_array_new<string>()
    for i in 0..prelude.len(items) {
        if is_star_item(items[i].expr) {
            for at in try star_positions(items[i].expr, names, tables) {
                push(headers, names[at])
            }
        } else {
            push(headers, item_name(items[i], i))
        }
    }
    return ok(snapshot(headers))
}

fn item_name(item: SelectItem, at: int) -> string {
    if item.alias != "" {
        return item.alias
    }
    return switch item.expr {
        Col(ref) { ref.name }
        Lit(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Param(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Not(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Bin(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        IsNull(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Call(node) { node.name }
        Sub(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Exists(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        InSub(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Cast(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Like(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        In(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Between(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Case(_) { prelude.format("column{}", at + 1) }
        Star { "*" }
        TableStar(table) { concat(table, ".*") }
    }
}

// A result cut down to its `LIMIT` and `OFFSET`, which is the last thing SQL does to a query --
// after the projection, after `DISTINCT`, after the ordering, and for a compound query after the
// whole compound rather than to any one arm. One function, because all three paths end here.
fn sliced_rows(
    rows: [Row],
    stmt: SelectStmt,
    database: Database,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool
) -> Result<[Row], DbError> {
    var from = 0
    if Some(e) = stmt.offset {
        from = unwrap_int(try eval_expr(e, bare_ctx(database, params, strict)))
    }
    var upto = prelude.len(rows)
    if Some(e) = stmt.limit {
        val count = unwrap_int(try eval_expr(e, bare_ctx(database, params, strict)))
        if count >= 0 && from + count < upto {
            upto = from + count
        }
    }
    if from > prelude.len(rows) {
        from = prelude.len(rows)
    }
    if upto < from {
        upto = from
    }
    val sliced = mutable_array_new<Row>()
    for k in from..upto {
        push(sliced, rows[k])
    }
    return ok(snapshot(sliced))
}

// A compound query: two or more results joined by `UNION`, `INTERSECT` or `EXCEPT`.
//
// **The grouping is the engine's decision, not the parser's, because the two dialects disagree
// about it.** `a UNION b INTERSECT c` is `(a UNION b) INTERSECT c` in SQLite, which reads all three
// operators strictly left to right, and `a UNION (b INTERSECT c)` in the standard and in
// PostgreSQL, which bind `INTERSECT` tighter than the other two. Both were measured, on real
// SQLite and on postgres:17, with a dataset that tells the two apart. So `sql` records the sequence
// as written and this function groups it -- the lax reading left to right, the strict reading with
// one pass over the `INTERSECT`s first.
//
// `ORDER BY`, `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` belong to the whole compound, and an `ORDER BY` here may name
// only an output column or its position: there is no single source to resolve anything else
// against, which is what both engines say in their own words.
fn run_compound(
    database: Database,
    stmt: SelectStmt,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool,
    outer: [Ctx]
) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val no_ops: [SetOp] = []
    val no_order: [OrderTerm] = []
    val head = with stmt {
        order: no_order,
        limit: Option<Expr>.None,
        offset: Option<Expr>.None,
        set_ops: no_ops
    }
    val columns = try query_columns(database, head, params, strict, outer)

    val operands = mutable_array_new<[[SqlValue]]>()
    push(operands, try result_values(database, head, params, strict, outer))
    val kinds = mutable_array_new<string>()
    val alls = mutable_array_new<bool>()
    for op in stmt.set_ops {
        // SQLite has no `INTERSECT ALL` or `EXCEPT ALL` -- it refuses them where it reads the
        // `ALL`, in those words -- while PostgreSQL has both. `sql` reads them because one of the
        // two engines does; the SQLite-shaped reading answers what SQLite answers, down to the
        // message, so the cross-check can report agreement rather than a near miss.
        val all = sql.set_op_keeps_all(op)
        if all && op.kind != "UNION" && !strict {
            return err(problem("near \"ALL\": syntax error"))
        }
        // SQLite has no `UNION DISTINCT` either, although `DISTINCT` is what a bare `UNION` means
        // and PostgreSQL accepts the word. Refused here for the same reason: this reading answers
        // what SQLite answers.
        if sql.fold_name(op.quantifier) == "distinct" && !strict {
            return err(problem("near \"DISTINCT\": syntax error"))
        }
        val written = if op.quantifier == "" { op.kind } else { concat(op.kind, " ", op.quantifier) }
        val arm_columns = try query_columns(database, op.query, params, strict, outer)
        if prelude.len(arm_columns) != prelude.len(columns) {
            return err(problem(prelude.format(
                "SELECTs to the left and right of {} do not have the same number of result columns",
                written
            )))
        }
        push(operands, try result_values(database, op.query, params, strict, outer))
        push(kinds, op.kind)
        push(alls, all)
    }

    var rows = try folded(snapshot(operands), snapshot(kinds), snapshot(alls), strict)

    if prelude.len(stmt.order) > 0 {
        val no_source: [string] = []
        val keys = try order_keys(stmt.order, columns, no_source)
        for k in 0..prelude.len(keys) {
            if keys[k].at < 0 {
                return err(problem(prelude.format(
                    "{} ORDER BY term does not match any column in the result set",
                    ordinal_word(k + 1)
                )))
            }
        }
        rows = sort_with(rows, fn(a: [SqlValue], b: [SqlValue]) -> int {
            for key in keys {
                val c = compare_values(a[key.at], b[key.at])
                if c != 0 {
                    return if key.descending { 0 - c } else { c }
                }
            }
            return 0
        })
    }

    val out = mutable_array_new<Row>()
    for values in rows {
        push(out, Row { columns: columns, values: values })
    }
    return ok(Outcome {
        database: database,
        rows: try sliced_rows(snapshot(out), stmt, database, params, strict),
        affected: 0
    })
}

// The operands folded into one result, in the order the reading in force gives them.
fn folded(operands: [[[SqlValue]]], kinds: [string], alls: [bool], strict: bool) -> Result<[[SqlValue]], DbError> {
    var left = operands
    var ops = kinds
    var all_flags = alls
    if strict {
        // One pass over the `INTERSECT`s, which bind tighter than `UNION` and `EXCEPT` in the
        // standard. What is left after it is a sequence of equal-precedence operators, folded
        // left to right like the lax reading.
        val tighter = mutable_array_new<[[SqlValue]]>()
        val rest_kinds = mutable_array_new<string>()
        val rest_alls = mutable_array_new<bool>()
        push(tighter, operands[0])
        for i in 1..prelude.len(operands) {
            if kinds[i - 1] == "INTERSECT" {
                val last = prelude.len(tighter) - 1
                tighter[last] = combine_results("INTERSECT", alls[i - 1], tighter[last], operands[i])
            } else {
                push(rest_kinds, kinds[i - 1])
                push(rest_alls, alls[i - 1])
                push(tighter, operands[i])
            }
        }
        left = snapshot(tighter)
        ops = snapshot(rest_kinds)
        all_flags = snapshot(rest_alls)
    }
    var rows = left[0]
    for i in 1..prelude.len(left) {
        rows = combine_results(ops[i - 1], all_flags[i - 1], rows, left[i])
    }
    return ok(rows)
}

// The output column names of a query, read from its shape rather than from its rows -- which is
// what makes the arity check work for an arm that happens to answer nothing.
fn query_columns(
    database: Database,
    q: SelectStmt,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool,
    outer: [Ctx]
) -> Result<[string], DbError> {
    val cx = try shape_ctx(database, q, outer, params, strict)
    return item_headers(q.items, cx.columns, cx.tables)
}

fn result_values(
    database: Database,
    q: SelectStmt,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool,
    outer: [Ctx]
) -> Result<[[SqlValue]], DbError> {
    val out = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for r in (try run_select(database, q, params, strict, outer)).rows {
        push(out, r.values)
    }
    return ok(snapshot(out))
}

fn run_select(
    database: Database,
    stmt: SelectStmt,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool,
    outer: [Ctx]
) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    // Before validation, because a compound's `ORDER BY` is resolved against the compound's output
    // and not against the head's source -- `validate_select` would refuse a legal one.
    if prelude.len(stmt.set_ops) > 0 {
        return run_compound(database, stmt, params, strict, outer)
    }
    try validate_select(database, stmt, outer, params, strict)
    val source = try build_source(database, stmt, params, strict, outer)
    val sc = Scope { database: database, source: source, params: params, strict: strict, outer: outer }
    val names = source.columns

    // The rows the `WHERE` keeps, as indexes into the source.
    val chosen_rows = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for i in 0..prelude.len(source.rows) {
        var keep = true
        if Some(e) = stmt.filter {
            keep = is_true(try eval_expr(e, scope_ctx(sc, source.rows[i])))
        }
        if keep {
            push(chosen_rows, i)
        }
    }
    val chosen = snapshot(chosen_rows)

    var aggregated = prelude.len(stmt.groups) > 0 || Option.is_Some(stmt.having)
    for item in stmt.items {
        if has_aggregate(item.expr) {
            aggregated = true
        }
    }
    if aggregated {
        return run_grouped(sc, stmt, chosen)
    }

    // A `*` expands to every column the `FROM` produced, joins included -- **anywhere in the
    // list**, so `SELECT *, id` is two copies of `id` and not an error. It was once allowed only on
    // its own, which the `RETURNING` cross-check found by running `RETURNING *, id` against real
    // SQLite.
    val columns = try item_headers(stmt.items, names, source.tables)

    // **Projected before ordered**, which is the order SQL specifies and what an `ORDER BY` naming
    // an output column or a position needs: `ORDER BY 2` has nothing to read until the second
    // output column exists.
    val values_of = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for i in chosen {
        val values = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
        for item in stmt.items {
            if is_star_item(item.expr) {
                for at in try star_positions(item.expr, names, source.tables) {
                    push(values, present(source.rows[i][at], strict))
                }
            } else {
                push(values, present(try eval_expr(item.expr, scope_ctx(sc, source.rows[i])), strict))
            }
        }
        push(values_of, snapshot(values))
    }
    val values_by_place = snapshot(values_of)

    // Sorted as a permutation of places, so a comparator can reach both the projected row and the
    // source row behind it -- a term that is neither an ordinal nor an output name is still an
    // expression over the source.
    val order = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for k in 0..prelude.len(chosen) {
        push(order, k)
    }
    var places = snapshot(order)
    if prelude.len(stmt.order) > 0 {
        val keys = try order_keys(stmt.order, columns, names)
        val failure = mutable_new<string>("")
        places = sort_with(places, fn(a: int, b: int) -> int {
            for key in keys {
                var left: Result<SqlValue, DbError> = ok(SqlValue.Null)
                var right: Result<SqlValue, DbError> = ok(SqlValue.Null)
                if key.at >= 0 {
                    left = ok(values_by_place[a][key.at])
                    right = ok(values_by_place[b][key.at])
                } else {
                    left = eval_expr(key.expr, scope_ctx(sc, source.rows[chosen[a]]))
                    right = eval_expr(key.expr, scope_ctx(sc, source.rows[chosen[b]]))
                }
                if Err(e) = left {
                    set(failure, db_message(e))
                    return 0
                }
                if Err(e) = right {
                    set(failure, db_message(e))
                    return 0
                }
                val c = compare_values(unwrap(left), unwrap(right))
                if c != 0 {
                    return if key.descending { 0 - c } else { c }
                }
            }
            return 0
        })
        if get(failure) != "" {
            return err(problem(get(failure)))
        }
    }

    val projected = mutable_array_new<Row>()
    for place in places {
        push(projected, Row { columns: columns, values: values_by_place[place] })
    }
    // `DISTINCT` drops duplicates *after* the projection and *before* `LIMIT`, which is the order
    // SQL specifies -- `SELECT DISTINCT x .. LIMIT 2` means two distinct rows, not the distinct
    // ones among the first two.
    var rows = snapshot(projected)
    if stmt.distinct {
        rows = distinct_rows(rows)
    }

    return ok(Outcome {
        database: database,
        rows: try sliced_rows(rows, stmt, database, params, strict),
        affected: 0
    })
}

// The rows a `FROM` clause produces, with a name and a table for each column.
//
// **A join is a chain, built left to right.** The first table's rows are the start; each join
// combines what is there with the next table's rows. `CROSS` keeps every pair, `INNER` keeps the
// pairs its `ON` accepts, and `LEFT` keeps every left row, padding with NULLs where the right side
// has nothing -- which is why its filter runs per left row rather than over the finished product.
//
// `RIGHT` and `FULL` are read by `sql` and refused here. They are a mirror and a union of what
// this does, and writing them without a test that proves them against a real engine would be
// guessing; the error says which engine to use instead.
struct Source {
    columns: [string]
    tables: [string]
    rows: [[SqlValue]]
}

// Everything a row of one query is evaluated against, bundled: the database a subquery may need,
// the shape the `FROM` produced, the statement's parameters, the reading of SQL in force, and the
// scopes enclosing this query. Threading five of these through four functions separately was the
// alternative.
struct Scope {
    database: Database
    source: Source
    params: [SqlValue]
    strict: bool
    outer: [Ctx]
}

// A context over one table's row, for the statements that read a single table: `matching`'s
// filter, an `UPDATE`'s assignments.
fn table_ctx(database: Database, t: Table, row: [SqlValue], params: [SqlValue], strict: bool) -> Ctx {
    val names = column_names(t)
    val tables = mutable_array_new<string>()
    for _ in names {
        push(tables, t.name)
    }
    return ctx_of(database, names, snapshot(tables), row, params, strict)
}

fn scope_ctx(sc: Scope, row: [SqlValue]) -> Ctx {
    var cx = ctx_of(sc.database, sc.source.columns, sc.source.tables, row, sc.params, sc.strict)
    for o in sc.outer {
        cx = nested_in(cx, o)
    }
    return cx
}

fn table_source(database: Database, ref: sql.TableRef) -> Result<Source, DbError> {
    val t = try table_of(database, ref.name)
    val names = column_names(t)
    val qualifier = if ref.alias == "" { t.name } else { ref.alias }
    val tables = mutable_array_new<string>()
    for _ in names {
        push(tables, qualifier)
    }
    return ok(Source { columns: names, tables: snapshot(tables), rows: t.rows })
}

// The pair of rows a join puts side by side, as one row.
fn combined(left: [SqlValue], right: [SqlValue]) -> [SqlValue] {
    return concat(left, right)
}

fn nulls_like(columns: [string]) -> [SqlValue] {
    val out = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
    for _ in columns {
        push(out, SqlValue.Null)
    }
    return snapshot(out)
}

// A `USING (a, b)` as the condition it stands for: the named column of the left side equals the
// named column of the right side, for each.
fn using_condition(using: [string], left: sql.TableRef, right: sql.TableRef) -> Option<Expr> {
    if prelude.len(using) == 0 {
        return Option<Expr>.None
    }
    val left_name = if left.alias == "" { left.name } else { left.alias }
    val right_name = if right.alias == "" { right.name } else { right.alias }
    var built = Option<Expr>.None
    for name in using {
        val one = Expr.Bin(BinNode {
            op: "=",
            left: Expr.Col(sql.ColRef { table: left_name, name: name }),
            right: Expr.Col(sql.ColRef { table: right_name, name: name })
        })
        built = switch built {
            Some(previous) { Option<Expr>.Some(Expr.Bin(BinNode { op: "AND", left: previous, right: one })) }
            None { Option<Expr>.Some(one) }
        }
    }
    return built
}

fn build_source(
    database: Database,
    stmt: SelectStmt,
    params: [SqlValue],
    strict: bool,
    outer: [Ctx]
) -> Result<Source, DbError> {
    if prelude.len(stmt.from) == 0 {
        val none: [string] = []
        // One row of no columns, which is what makes `SELECT 1` answer a single 1.
        val no_values: [SqlValue] = []
        return ok(Source { columns: none, tables: none, rows: [no_values] })
    }
    var source = try table_source(database, stmt.from[0])
    var previous = stmt.from[0]
    for j in stmt.joins {
        if j.kind == "RIGHT" || j.kind == "FULL" {
            return err(problem(concat(
                j.kind,
                " JOIN is read by `sql` and not run by this engine; use sqlite or postgres for it"
            )))
        }
        val right = try table_source(database, j.table)
        val columns = concat(source.columns, right.columns)
        val tables = concat(source.tables, right.tables)
        val condition = switch j.on {
            Some(e) { Option<Expr>.Some(e) }
            None { using_condition(j.using, previous, j.table) }
        }
        val rows = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
        for l in source.rows {
            var matched = false
            for r in right.rows {
                val row = combined(l, r)
                var keep = true
                if Some(e) = condition {
                    var cx = ctx_of(database, columns, tables, row, params, strict)
                    for o in outer {
                        cx = nested_in(cx, o)
                    }
                    keep = is_true(try eval_expr(e, cx))
                }
                if keep {
                    matched = true
                    push(rows, row)
                }
            }
            if j.kind == "LEFT" && !matched {
                push(rows, combined(l, nulls_like(right.columns)))
            }
        }
        source = Source { columns: columns, tables: tables, rows: snapshot(rows) }
        previous = j.table
    }
    return ok(source)
}

// A subquery, run against its enclosing scope so a correlated one can read the outer row.
fn run_subquery(query: SelectStmt, cx: Ctx) -> Result<[Row], DbError> {
    val outer = push(cx.outer, cx)
    return ok((try run_select(cx.database, query, cx.params, cx.strict, outer)).rows)
}

// `DISTINCT`: the first of each repeated row survives, in the order it first appeared. Compared by
// SQL's own rules, so two NULLs count as the same row -- `DISTINCT` asks about distinctness, not
// about equality, the same reading `GROUP BY` takes of its keys.
fn distinct_rows(rows: [Row]) -> [Row] {
    val out = mutable_array_new<Row>()
    for r in rows {
        var seen = false
        for kept in out {
            if !seen && values_same(kept.values, r.values) {
                seen = true
            }
        }
        if !seen {
            push(out, r)
        }
    }
    return snapshot(out)
}

// Two rows compared the way SQL compares them for *distinctness*: **two NULLs are the same row**,
// although `NULL = NULL` is unknown. `DISTINCT`, `GROUP BY`'s keys and every set operation take
// this same reading, which is why it is one function.
fn values_same(a: [SqlValue], b: [SqlValue]) -> bool {
    if prelude.len(a) != prelude.len(b) {
        return false
    }
    for i in 0..prelude.len(a) {
        if is_null(a[i]) != is_null(b[i]) {
            return false
        }
        if !is_null(a[i]) && compare_values(a[i], b[i]) != 0 {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}

fn occurrences(rows: [[SqlValue]], row: [SqlValue]) -> int {
    var n = 0
    for other in rows {
        if values_same(other, row) {
            n = n + 1
        }
    }
    return n
}

fn distinct_values(rows: [[SqlValue]]) -> [[SqlValue]] {
    val out = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for r in rows {
        if occurrences(snapshot(out), r) == 0 {
            push(out, r)
        }
    }
    return snapshot(out)
}

// One set operation applied to two results.
//
// `UNION` concatenates, `INTERSECT` keeps what is in both, `EXCEPT` keeps what is only in the left
// -- each of them removing duplicates unless `ALL` was written, in which case **counts matter**:
// `INTERSECT ALL` keeps a row as many times as it appears in the side it appears in least, and
// `EXCEPT ALL` removes one occurrence per occurrence on the right. Measured against PostgreSQL,
// which is where the `ALL` forms of the two come from at all.
fn combine_results(kind: string, all: bool, left: [[SqlValue]], right: [[SqlValue]]) -> [[SqlValue]] {
    if kind == "UNION" {
        val joined = concat(left, right)
        return if all { joined } else { distinct_values(joined) }
    }
    val out = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    if kind == "INTERSECT" {
        val source = if all { left } else { distinct_values(left) }
        for r in source {
            val available = occurrences(right, r)
            if all {
                if occurrences(snapshot(out), r) < available {
                    push(out, r)
                }
            } else if available > 0 {
                push(out, r)
            }
        }
        return snapshot(out)
    }
    // EXCEPT
    val source = if all { left } else { distinct_values(left) }
    val dropped = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for r in source {
        val to_drop = occurrences(right, r)
        if all {
            if occurrences(snapshot(dropped), r) < to_drop {
                push(dropped, r)
            } else {
                push(out, r)
            }
        } else if to_drop == 0 {
            push(out, r)
        }
    }
    return snapshot(out)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- ordering

// Where an `ORDER BY` term's value comes from. `at` is a position in the *output* row, for a term
// that is an ordinal (`ORDER BY 2`) or the name the select list gave a column (`ORDER BY alias`);
// `at` is -1 for a term to be evaluated per row, which is every other term.
struct OrderKey {
    at: int
    expr: Expr
    descending: bool
}

// `1st`, `2nd`, `3rd`, `4th`, `11th`, `21st` -- SQLite's wording for an out-of-range `ORDER BY`
// term names the term this way, and matching it exactly is what lets the cross-check fixture
// report agreement instead of a near miss.
fn ordinal_word(n: int) -> string {
    val last_two = n % 100
    val suffix = if last_two >= 11 && last_two <= 13 {
        "th"
    } else if n % 10 == 1 {
        "st"
    } else if n % 10 == 2 {
        "nd"
    } else if n % 10 == 3 {
        "rd"
    } else {
        "th"
    }
    return concat(str(n), suffix)
}

// How each `ORDER BY` term will be read, resolved once before any row is sorted.
//
// **A term resolves against the source first and the output second**, which is the order SQL gives
// it: `SELECT name FROM t ORDER BY name` orders by the column, not by the output column that
// happens to share its name. An integer literal is a *position* in the output row rather than the
// number it looks like -- `ORDER BY 1` sorts, where evaluating the literal per row would compare 1
// with 1 for every pair and silently leave the rows as they came. That is what this engine did
// until set operations needed ordering by position, and `SELECT n FROM a ORDER BY 1 DESC` answered
// ascending where SQLite answers descending.
fn order_keys(terms: [OrderTerm], columns: [string], source_columns: [string]) -> Result<[OrderKey], DbError> {
    val out = mutable_array_new<OrderKey>()
    for k in 0..prelude.len(terms) {
        val term = terms[k]
        var at = 0 - 1
        if Lit(v) = term.expr {
            if Int(n) = v {
                if n < 1 || n > prelude.len(columns) {
                    return err(problem(prelude.format(
                        "{} ORDER BY term out of range - should be between 1 and {}",
                        ordinal_word(k + 1),
                        prelude.len(columns)
                    )))
                }
                at = n - 1
            }
        }
        if at < 0 {
            if Col(ref) = term.expr {
                if ref.table == "" && column_index(source_columns, ref.name) < 0 {
                    at = column_index(columns, ref.name)
                }
            }
        }
        push(out, OrderKey { at: at, expr: term.expr, descending: term.descending })
    }
    return ok(snapshot(out))
}

// The grouped form of `run_select`: partition, fold, filter, order, slice, project.
//
// With no `GROUP BY` there is **one** group holding every matching row -- including when there are
// none, which is what makes `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM empty` answer one row of 0 rather than no rows
// at all. With a `GROUP BY` and no matching rows there are no groups and no rows, which is the
// other half of the same rule.
fn run_grouped(sc: Scope, stmt: SelectStmt, chosen: [int]) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val database = sc.database
    val source = sc.source
    val params = sc.params
    val strict = sc.strict

    val keys = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    val members = mutable_array_new<[[SqlValue]]>()
    if prelude.len(stmt.groups) == 0 {
        val all = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
        for i in chosen {
            push(all, source.rows[i])
        }
        val no_key: [SqlValue] = []
        push(keys, no_key)
        push(members, snapshot(all))
    } else {
        for i in chosen {
            val row = source.rows[i]
            val key = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
            for g in stmt.groups {
                push(key, try eval_expr(g, scope_ctx(sc, row)))
            }
            val wanted = snapshot(key)
            var at = 0 - 1
            for k in 0..prelude.len(keys) {
                if at < 0 {
                    var same = true
                    for j in 0..prelude.len(wanted) {
                        // Two NULL keys group together, although `NULL = NULL` is unknown:
                        // grouping compares for distinctness, not with the equality operator.
                        val a = keys[k][j]
                        val b = wanted[j]
                        if is_null(a) != is_null(b) {
                            same = false
                        } else if !is_null(a) && compare_values(a, b) != 0 {
                            same = false
                        }
                    }
                    if same {
                        at = k
                    }
                }
            }
            if at < 0 {
                push(keys, wanted)
                push(members, [row])
            } else {
                members[at] = push(members[at], row)
            }
        }
    }
    val grouped = snapshot(members)

    val surviving = mutable_array_new<[[SqlValue]]>()
    for rows in grouped {
        var keep = true
        if Some(e) = stmt.having {
            keep = is_true(try eval_grouped(e, sc, rows))
        }
        if keep {
            push(surviving, rows)
        }
    }
    val groups_left = snapshot(surviving)

    val columns = try item_headers(stmt.items, source.columns, source.tables)
    // Projected before ordered here too, so `ORDER BY 2` and `ORDER BY alias` mean in a grouped
    // query what they mean in a plain one.
    val values_of = mutable_array_new<[SqlValue]>()
    for rows in groups_left {
        val values = mutable_array_new<SqlValue>()
        for item in stmt.items {
            push(values, present(try eval_grouped(item.expr, sc, rows), strict))
        }
        push(values_of, snapshot(values))
    }
    val values_by_place = snapshot(values_of)

    val order = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for k in 0..prelude.len(groups_left) {
        push(order, k)
    }
    var places = snapshot(order)
    if prelude.len(stmt.order) > 0 {
        val keys = try order_keys(stmt.order, columns, source.columns)
        val failure = mutable_new<string>("")
        places = sort_with(places, fn(a: int, b: int) -> int {
            for key in keys {
                var left: Result<SqlValue, DbError> = ok(SqlValue.Null)
                var right: Result<SqlValue, DbError> = ok(SqlValue.Null)
                if key.at >= 0 {
                    left = ok(values_by_place[a][key.at])
                    right = ok(values_by_place[b][key.at])
                } else {
                    left = eval_grouped(key.expr, sc, groups_left[a])
                    right = eval_grouped(key.expr, sc, groups_left[b])
                }
                if Err(e) = left {
                    set(failure, db_message(e))
                    return 0
                }
                if Err(e) = right {
                    set(failure, db_message(e))
                    return 0
                }
                val c = compare_values(unwrap(left), unwrap(right))
                if c != 0 {
                    return if key.descending { 0 - c } else { c }
                }
            }
            return 0
        })
        if get(failure) != "" {
            return err(problem(get(failure)))
        }
    }

    val projected = mutable_array_new<Row>()
    for place in places {
        push(projected, Row { columns: columns, values: values_by_place[place] })
    }
    var out_rows = snapshot(projected)
    if stmt.distinct {
        out_rows = distinct_rows(out_rows)
    }

    return ok(Outcome {
        database: database,
        rows: try sliced_rows(out_rows, stmt, database, params, strict),
        affected: 0
    })
}

fn db_message(e: DbError) -> string {
    return switch e {
        NotFound(m) { m }
        Constraint(m) { m }
        Connection(m) { m }
        Sql(m) { m }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the connection

// A connection's state: the database, and the database as it was when a transaction began.
//
// **This is where a database being a value pays for itself.** `BEGIN` keeps the current `Database`,
// `ROLLBACK` puts it back and `COMMIT` forgets it -- no undo log, no copying, because the saved
// value *is* the old state. The same property is what will make per-test forking free: seed a
// schema and a dataset once, then hand each test its own copy.
struct State {
    database: Mutable<Database>
    saved: Mutable<[Database]>
    // Which reading of SQL this connection takes: see `Ctx.strict` and the two drivers below.
    strict: bool
}

fn run_one(state: State, sql: string, params: [SqlValue]) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val stmt = try sql.parse(sql)
    val before = get(state.database)
    return switch stmt {
        Begin {
            set(state.saved, push(get(state.saved), before))
            ok(Outcome { database: before, rows: [], affected: 0 })
        }
        Commit {
            val depth = prelude.len(get(state.saved))
            if depth > 0 {
                set(state.saved, slice(get(state.saved), 0, depth - 1))
            }
            ok(Outcome { database: before, rows: [], affected: 0 })
        }
        Rollback {
            val stack = get(state.saved)
            val depth = prelude.len(stack)
            if depth == 0 {
                ok(Outcome { database: before, rows: [], affected: 0 })
            } else {
                set(state.saved, slice(stack, 0, depth - 1))
                ok(Outcome { database: stack[depth - 1], rows: [], affected: 0 })
            }
        }
        // A statement this engine has nothing to do about: a setting, an empty statement, or an
        // index, which decides how fast a query runs and not what it answers.
        Passthrough(_) { ok(Outcome { database: before, rows: [], affected: 0 }) }
        CreateIndex(_) { ok(Outcome { database: before, rows: [], affected: 0 }) }
        Create(s) { run_create(before, s) }
        Alter(s) { run_alter(before, s, state.strict) }
        Drop(s) { run_drop(before, s) }
        Insert(s) { run_insert(before, s, params, state.strict) }
        Update(s) { run_update(before, s, params, state.strict) }
        Delete(s) { run_delete(before, s, params, state.strict) }
        Select(s) {
            val no_outer: [Ctx] = []
            run_select(before, s, params, state.strict, no_outer)
        }
    }
}

// Runs `sql` and keeps whatever it changed. A statement that fails leaves the database untouched,
// because the new value is only published when the whole statement succeeded.
fn apply(state: State, sql: string, params: [SqlValue]) -> Result<Outcome, DbError> {
    val outcome = try run_one(state, sql, params)
    set(state.database, outcome.database)
    return ok(outcome)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the driver

// This engine as a `db.Driver`, for `datasource.registry([memdb.driver()])`.
//
// The URL is `memdb:` with anything after it ignored, so `memdb:` and `memdb:whatever` are both a
// fresh empty database. There is no sharing between connections and no file: like
// `sqlite::memory:`, a connection *is* the database, which is why `poolable` says no -- a pool of
// four would be four different databases.
pub fn driver() -> Driver {
    return engine("memdb:", false)
}

// The same engine reading SQL the way the **standard** does, at `memdb-ansi:`. Nine things differ,
// and the first five are places where SQLite departs from ANSI:
//
//   - `LIKE` is case-sensitive, as the standard and PostgreSQL have it, where SQLite folds ASCII;
//   - division by zero is an error (SQLSTATE 22012's rule), where SQLite answers NULL;
//   - comparing a number with text is a type error, where SQLite orders them by a type rank;
//   - a boolean in a select list stays a boolean, where SQLite has no boolean type and answers 0
//     or 1;
//   - a `PRIMARY KEY` column may not hold NULL, which makes a key implicitly `NOT NULL` as the
//     standard says -- where SQLite allows it, and for a single-column `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` fills
//     in the next rowid instead.
//
// The other four point the other way, SQLite being the stricter engine (each measured, not
// assumed):
//
//   - a chain of set operations groups left to right in SQLite and binds `INTERSECT` tighter in the
//     standard and in PostgreSQL, so `a UNION b INTERSECT c` is two different queries;
//   - `INTERSECT ALL`, `EXCEPT ALL` and `UNION DISTINCT` are syntax errors in SQLite and ordinary
//     SQL in PostgreSQL;
//   - `RETURNING t.*` is refused by SQLite and answered by PostgreSQL;
//   - `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN u TEXT UNIQUE` is refused by SQLite, which does not rewrite the
//     rows already there, and accepted by PostgreSQL, which does.
//
// For those, the lax reading refuses and this one runs them.
//
// **It is a portability check, not a better engine.** A suite that passes on `memdb:` and fails on
// `memdb-ansi:` has found SQL that leans on SQLite, which matters when the same application also
// ships on PostgreSQL -- and finding it costs a second local run rather than a Postgres container.
// A statement that fails the other way round, on `memdb:` only, is the mirror of that: SQL only
// PostgreSQL will take.
// Both report the dialect `memdb`, so migrations and `migrate`'s type tokens work for either.
pub fn strict_driver() -> Driver {
    return engine("memdb-ansi:", true)
}

fn engine(prefix: string, strict: bool) -> Driver {
    return Driver {
        name: "memdb",
        prefixes: [prefix],
        poolable: fn(url: string) -> bool { return false },
        // Nothing here can go stale: there is no socket and no file, only a value in this process.
        idle_check: false,
        open: fn(url: string) -> Result<Conn, DbError> {
            val state = State {
                database: mutable_new(empty_database()),
                saved: mutable_new<[Database]>([]),
                strict: strict
            }
            return ok(Conn {
                dialect: "memdb",
                close: fn() {},
                execute: fn(sql: string, params: [SqlValue]) -> Result<int, DbError> {
                    return ok((try apply(state, sql, params)).affected)
                },
                query: fn(sql: string, params: [SqlValue]) -> Result<[Row], DbError> {
                    return ok((try apply(state, sql, params)).rows)
                },
                rows: fn(sql: string, params: [SqlValue]) -> Result<Iter<Result<Row, DbError>>, DbError> {
                    // Every row is already in memory, so the lazy form hands them out one at a
                    // time from the array rather than pretending to be a cursor.
                    val all = (try apply(state, sql, params)).rows
                    val at = mutable_new(0)
                    return ok(iter_from<Result<Row, DbError>>(
                        fn() -> Option<Result<Row, DbError>> {
                            val i = get(at)
                            if i >= prelude.len(all) {
                                return Option<Result<Row, DbError>>.None
                            }
                            set(at, i + 1)
                            return Option<Result<Row, DbError>>.Some(ok(all[i]))
                        },
                        fn() {}
                    ))
                }
            })
        }
    }
}
